@node README "About NovaDock and installation"
@toc Main
@{b}About NovaDock and installation@{ub}
===============================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Features " link README 26}
  @{" Requirements " link README 42}
  @{" Build " link README 49}
  @{" Building an Amiga LHA release " link README 68}
  @{" Installing on AmigaOS " link README 126}
  @{" Plugin development " link README 206}
  @{" License " link README 212}
  @{" Maintainer release tool " link README 262}

NovaDock is a lightweight, extensible dock for Classic AmigaOS Workbench
3.x.

  * Website: novadock.retrocs.org (https://novadock.retrocs.org)
  * Source: github.com/retrocs-org/novadock
    (https://github.com/retrocs-org/novadock)

Fresh installations start with the Nord theme and six tiles: the boot disk
(SYS:), the installation's default shell (NewShell), Music, CPU, RAM and
Clock. Add other tiles or open their settings from the dock's right-click
menus. Updates keep existing settings; choose Reset in the installer to use
this layout.

@{b}Features@{ub}
--------

  * Application, command, drawer, and volume launchers with Workbench icons.
  * Application groups, normal and tiny layouts, multiple rows, themes,
    auto-hide, and screen-edge placement.
  * Running-application indicators, tooltips, drag-and-drop, and drawer
    popups.
  * Bundled CPU, memory, clock, music, archive, Directory Opus 4, and
    running-app plugins.
  * A unified Preferences application with Plugin Manager, organization,
    diagnostics, and About pages.
  * Recoverable atomic configuration saving, adaptive plugin pacing, and
    dock-capacity diagnostics.
  * A documented plugin SDK with a small example plugin.

@{b}Requirements@{ub}
------------

  * AmigaOS 3.0 or newer.
  * A classic 68k Amiga.
  * Optional plugin dependencies are described in the files under docs/.

@{b}Build@{ub}
-----

Install vbcc with an AmigaOS m68k target and a compatible Amiga NDK, then
run:

    sh scripts/build-amiga.sh -B

To build and copy the required Amiga files into a shared development
directory:

    sh scripts/build-and-stage-amiga.sh "/path/to/shared/bin"

The staging script creates a NovaDock subdirectory at the destination. Its
outer NovaDock.info sets Workbench to Show Only Icons and View By Icon, so
files without icons stay out of the normal package view. On AmigaOS,
double-click @{b}Install-NovaDock@{ub} in that drawer to install with the standard
Installer, or start the programs directly from it.

@{b}Building an Amiga LHA release@{ub}
-----------------------------

Configure VBCC for your vbcc installation and LHA for @{b}LHa for UNIX@{ub} (the
archiver that can create archives; lhasa only extracts), then run:

    sh scripts/make-release.sh /path/to/releases

The script regenerates the manual/catalog definitions, rebuilds all Amiga
programs, stages a clean installer package and creates NovaDock-0.1.lha, its
SHA-256 checksum and an accompanying readme. The archive contains the
NovaDock installation drawer and its outer NovaDock.info, including the
installer and prebuilt icons. It uses LH5 compression with level-1 headers,
tests CRCs, extracts the archive and compares every file before replacing a
previous release.

VC can override the compiler command for a custom vbcc configuration.
ND_SKIP_BUILD=1 packages existing binaries when a rebuild has already
completed. UPLOADER optionally fills the readme's uploader field. Normal
builds never upload anything.

@{b}Preparing an Aminet submission@{ub}

    UPLOADER="your-email\@example.com" AUTHOR="Your name" \\
      sh scripts/make-release.sh /path/to/releases --aminet

This builds the usual versioned release and prepares aminet/novadock.lha,
aminet/novadock.readme and a local submission.json containing hashes. This
command only prepares and previews files; it makes no connection to Aminet.
Set your real uploader email when you are ready. The readme is wrapped to 78
columns, with required metadata and a Short field of at most 40 characters.

Review the prepared files, then run a local check:

    python3 tools/aminet_release.py check /path/to/releases/aminet

Only when ready to submit, run the same command with --upload.
Alternatively, make-release.sh /path/to/releases --upload-aminet explicitly
builds, prepares and submits in one invocation (with UPLOADER set). Upload
transfers only the archive and matching readme, with the readme last. The
manifest and checksums stay local. Submission goes to Aminet incoming;
publication depends on Aminet processing. No upload is performed by the
default build or by double-clicking the local Mac launcher.

Aminet recommends a stable filename, so its copy is novadock.lha with the
version in the readme; local releases remain versioned. For replacing older
differently named releases, set AMINET_REPLACES to full Aminet paths
separated by semicolons, for example util/wb/NovaDock-0.1.lha. Omit it for
the first release or updates to the same novadock.lha filename. Edited or
rebuilt prepared files fail the hash check; prepare the submission again
before uploading.

See Aminet's upload instructions
(https://wiki.aminet.net/Uploading_instructions) and readme format
(https://wiki.aminet.net/The_Readme_file). The default target is anonymous
FTP at main.aminet.net/new, as documented there. Recheck their instructions
before a future public release.

@{b}Installing on AmigaOS@{ub}
---------------------

@{b}Install-NovaDock@{ub} is a GUI installer for the Commodore Installer included
with AmigaOS 3.x (version 42 or later). @{b}Novice@{ub} is selected by default: it
uses the standard paths, enables startup with Workbench and opens NovaDock
after installation, without asking for these choices. Existing settings are
kept, and updates retain recorded installation paths.

@{b}Average (Intermediate)@{ub} asks for the application drawer (default
SYS:Utilities) and Preferences drawer (default SYS:Prefs). Each binary and
its .info file goes directly into the selected drawer. Shared components use
SYS:NovaDock.

@{b}Expert@{ub} also asks for the shared-data parent (default SYS:), where a NovaDock
drawer holds plugins, artwork, themes, scripts and helper tools. Help,
settings and translations use their standard locations in every mode.

Both Average and Expert ask two separate Yes/No questions, in order: start
NovaDock automatically with Workbench, then start NovaDock when installation
finishes. Updates retain recorded locations in every mode.

The dock's messages, menus and calendar are translated into German and
Swedish; the installer adds these translations to LOCALE:Catalogs, and
NovaDock uses the language chosen in Locale preferences.

The AmigaGuide manual, NovaDock.guide, is always installed as
HELP:NovaDock.guide: press @{b}Help@{ub} in any NovaDock Preferences window, or the
@{b}Help...@{ub} button on its About page, to open it in MultiView.

Existing settings are kept unless you choose to reset them (the old file is
saved as novadock.cfg.old). Run the installer again to update NovaDock,
keeping your settings and imported plugins and themes, or to remove it. A
running NovaDock is stopped first: NovaDock QUIT asks a running dock to quit
from the Shell.

The Workbench icon uses the bundled StartInstaller launcher. It prefers
SYS:System/Installer, falls back to C:Installer, and runs with a 64 KB
stack. Keep the launcher beside Install-NovaDock. Uninstall progress is
recorded in ENVARC:NovaDock-Uninstall.log so the last completed step can be
checked after a restart.

The Shell script install.amiga installs the standard locations. Use the GUI
installer to select custom locations. The installer records NovaDock:,
NovaDockApp: and NovaDockPrefs: in S:User-Startup. Help uses HELP: without
an extra assignment. Updates preserve imported components. The old Utilities
drawer is backed up before the application replaces it.

Adding, moving and removing tiles updates the existing dock window when its
window resources and fitted tile dimensions remain compatible. Existing
scaled artwork and plugin state are reused; only new artwork is loaded.
Changes to the theme, font, refresh mode or fitted tile size use the full
rebuild path.

The optional @{"BitSkim plugin" link BITSKIM_PLUGIN} starts or restores BitSkim, shows nearby
computers, incoming offers, transfer speed and retained results. Right-click
Send file or drop one file to choose a recipient using protocol 2; Open
BitSkim for detailed transfer information. BitSkim is installed separately.

The optional @{"AmIRC plugin" link AMIRC_PLUGIN} shows IRC client status and can start or restore
AmIRC. Add it through the dock background menu.

@{"Settings saves and Diagnostics" link RESPONSIVE_SETTINGS} now separate background file writes from live
dock updates.

Network replaces the standalone Roadshow plugin and combines its controls
with packet meters/graphs in one tile. Its right-click menu offers Online or
Offline, a Details window, and settings; the main Network interface selector
derives its driver/unit automatically. Sampling overrides require explicit
opt-in under Advanced. Failed connection changes open an error popup. See
@{"Network" link NETWORK_PLUGIN}, including requirements for compatible TCP/IP stacks.

@{"Clippy" link CLIPBOARD_PLUGIN} offers a centered picker and a separate management window.
@{"AmIRC unread alerts" link AMIRC_PLUGIN} use numbered badges through the supplied event hook.
Repeated Apps clicks keep its running-application popup open.

After manually replacing executables, quit and start NovaDock to replace
resident plugin processes as well. The installer handles stopping the dock
for an update; copying binaries alone does not update a running process.

@{b}Plugin development@{ub}
------------------

Start with @{"the Plugin SDK quick start" link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART}. The complete protocol is documented
in @{"the Plugin API" link PLUGIN_API}.

@{b}License@{ub}
-------

NovaDock is released under the MIT License.

Network full-stack shutdown first puts the selected Roadshow interface
offline, then runs NetShutdown. The native bridged A2065 comparison
completed with this sequence; direct NetShutdown hung with the dock stopped.
A failed interface step produces an error and leaves full shutdown
unstarted. Network uses separate IN/OUT meters by default; online clicks
still switch to the two-channel graph.

Default installed layout:

  * SYS:Utilities/NovaDock and .info: visible application.
  * SYS:Prefs/NovaDockPrefs and .info: visible Preferences application.
  * SYS:NovaDock/: shared data, with no drawer icon. Plugins, Themes, Icons,
    Rexx, and Tools live here; Tools/SaveConfig writes saved settings.
  * HELP:NovaDock.guide and .info: the manual goes directly into HELP:,
    without a subdrawer. The default tool is SYS:Utilities/MultiView.
  * ENV:NovaDock/novadock.cfg: active settings; Use updates ENV.
  * ENVARC:NovaDock/novadock.cfg: permanent settings; Save updates ENV and
    ENVARC. Settings drawers have icons, and the existing config
    filename/format is preserved.

Only the application drawer, Preferences drawer and shared-data parent are
selectable. Help, settings and translations use their standard assignments.
The shared directory has no .info, so it stays out of Workbench's normal
icon view; Show All Files can still reveal it. The unpacked distribution
retains its drawer and installer icons.

When an installed executable is detected, every user level, including
Novice, displays its locations and offers Update or Uninstall. Use the
Installer's Abort Install button to cancel. Uninstall always asks for
confirmation and whether to keep settings; Novice does not bypass these
questions. Update is selected by default, but the choice is always shown.
Updates retain custom binary/shared-data locations, migrate the earlier
default shared-data location to SYS:NovaDock, and move older manuals
directly into HELP:. Imported plugins and themes are preserved. Leftover
resources alone do not count as an installed application. Uninstall
automatically resolves recorded application, Preferences and shared-data
locations, falling back to documented standard and legacy directories. It
never asks you to select directories, including when the Plugins drawer is
missing but Themes or Tools remains. It only asks for removal confirmation
and whether to keep settings. Uninstall deletes only NovaDock's files from
old shared Help locations and never removes the HELP: root.

BitSkim defaults to SYS:Utilities/BitSkim/BitSkim; custom executable paths
remain configurable. The installer stays in the distribution package.

@{b}Maintainer release tool@{ub}
-----------------------

Compile, test, package and publish the same tagged release to GitHub and
GitLab with python3 scripts/release.py (plan, build, check, prepare,
confirmed publish and separate source push). Aminet submission is supported
by the same tool. See @{"docs/RELEASING.md" link RELEASING} for setup and commands.

The standalone plugin SDK source tree is in @{"plugin-sdk/" link README}.
@endnode

@node LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS "Launchers and icons"
@toc Main
@{b}Launchers and icons@{ub}
===================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Preferences UI " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 17}
  @{" Text configuration " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 59}
  @{" Unified Launcher Action " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 78}
  @{" Bundled icons " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 136}
  @{" Installation or upgrades " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 173}
  @{" Dock context menu and direct manipulation (NovaDock 0.1) " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 183}
  @{" Later 0.1: tooltips, Workbench drops, ARexx " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 279}
  @{" Stack, quoting and running applications " link LAUNCHERS_AND_ICONS 285}

NovaDock supports separate @{b}application/command@{ub} launchers and @{b}Workbench
folder/drawer@{ub} launchers. Both use the optional third field for a custom
icon.

@{b}Preferences UI@{ub}
--------------

Open SYS:Prefs/NovaDockPrefs (or PROGDIR:NovaDockPrefs from an unpacked
development tree). @{b}Organize@{ub} shows the active dock in explicit row groups. It
combines plugins, applications, drawers and spacers; Remove changes active
membership, Up/Down changes order and automatically crosses a row boundary,
and Edit opens the selected plugin or launcher editor. Add items with
Organize's @{b}Add...@{ub} button, from the dock's @{b}Add item@{ub} window, or by dragging a
Workbench object onto the dock. Both buttons open the same editor. The Dock
rows selector advances from one to three rows before cycling back to one;
Test, Use or Save applies the resulting arrangement.

Use @{b}Items@{ub} to create or edit reusable application and drawer definitions:

  * @{b}Add App@{ub} adds an application/AmigaDOS command tile.
  * @{b}Add Dir@{ub} adds a Workbench directory tile.
  * @{b}Name@{ub}, @{b}Target@{ub}, @{b}Icon@{ub} edit the current entry; choose the application or
    directory type and press @{b}Update@{ub} to commit it in the editor.
  * @{b}Icon...@{ub} uses the native ASL file requester; select an existing Workbench
    .info or IFF/ILBM .iff icon. The Icon string field also accepts any
    custom path (absolute with an Amiga assignment or relative to NovaDock).
  * @{b}Target...@{ub} opens an ASL file requester for applications, or a
    directory-only requester for drawer tiles (AmigaOS asl.library V38+).
  * @{b}Running task@{ub}, @{b}Window pattern@{ub}, and @{b}ARexx port@{ub} optionally identify the
    launched application for its status indicator and click-to-front. Leave
    all three empty for automatic command-basename detection.
  * @{b}Remove@{ub} deletes the selected definition. Use/Save commits text
    configuration and sends a live reload to the running dock. Closing the
    Preferences window does not persist edits.

Choosing another launcher, adding a definition, navigating to another
section, or accepting a Workbench drop automatically keeps valid edits in
the shared Preferences session. Invalid fields keep the current launcher
selected and remain on screen for correction. @{b}Update@{ub} is still available for
an explicit commit without leaving the current row.

The dock and configuration permit up to @{b}24 items@{ub}, including plugin tiles.
Overflow paging keeps every active item reachable on smaller screens.
NovaDock checks available Workbench screen space before accepting a Save or
Use.

@{b}Text configuration@{ub}
------------------

    # Application/AmigaDOS command -- old short lines remain valid
    LAUNCH=Shell|NewShell|Icons/Workbench.iff|ON
    LAUNCH=Music|SYS:Utilities/EaglePlayer|Icons/Games.iff
    LAUNCH=Preferences|SYS:Prefs/ScreenMode|SYS:Prefs/ScreenMode.info
    
    # Extended unified Launcher Action
    # name|target|icon|state|mode|arguments|start-in|hot-key|Tools-menu|free-space|task|window|port
    LAUNCH=Editor|C:Ed||OFF|CLI|S:Startup-Sequence|SYS:|control alt e|YES|YES
    LAUNCH=Player|rexx_EP||OFF|AREXX|PLAY 1||control alt p|NO|YES
    LAUNCH=EaglePlayer|SYS:Utilities/EaglePlayer||ON|AUTO||||NO|YES|EaglePlayer|EaglePlayer*|rexx_EP
    
    # Drawer/Workbench directory -- last field is the icon (optional)
    DRAWER=Games|Games:|Icons/Games.iff
    DRAWER=Utilities|SYS:Utilities|Icons/Drawer.iff
    DRAWER=Internet|SYS:Tools|Icons/Internet.iff

@{b}Unified Launcher Action@{ub}
-----------------------

Every application or directory definition is now one reusable action. The
same entry may be enabled in @{b}Organize@{ub} and assigned a Commodities-compatible
global @{b}Hot key@{ub}. An item does not have to occupy dock space for its hot key
to remain active. Older configurations may retain a Workbench Tools-menu
flag; NovaDock preserves that compatibility field, but no longer shows the
ambiguous Tools checkbox in Preferences.

The @{b}Run as@{ub} selector supports:

  * @{b}Automatic@{ub} -- preserves the original behavior: a real object is opened by
    Workbench when possible, with AmigaDOS as the fallback.
  * @{b}Workbench@{ub} -- explicitly uses OpenWorkbenchObject() on Workbench 3.5+ and
    falls back to launching the object path on earlier systems.
  * @{b}CLI@{ub} -- runs Target plus Arguments asynchronously. @{b}Start in@{ub} changes
    directory before invoking the command.
  * @{b}ARexx@{ub} -- Target is the public ARexx port and Arguments is the command.
    NovaDock sends the normal request and reports an error if the port is
    absent or returns a failure. Sending to NovaDock's own NOVADOCK port is
    rejected to avoid a self-request deadlock.

Hot-key descriptions use the standard Commodities syntax, for example
control alt e or shift f10. Invalid descriptions stay saved so they can be
corrected, but Commodities will not activate them. Hot keys require
commodities.library; all other launcher behavior continues if it is absent.

@{b}Running-application matching@{ub}

The three optional identity fields are alternatives and may be combined:

  * @{b}Running task@{ub} is the exact Exec task name shown by tools such as Scout.
  * @{b}Window pattern@{ub} is matched case-insensitively against Intuition window
    titles. * matches any sequence and ? matches one character; for example,
    EaglePlayer* matches versioned EaglePlayer titles.
  * @{b}ARexx port@{ub} is the exact public port name, such as rexx_EP. Its owning
    task is also used to locate and bring a window forward.

The launcher is considered running when any configured identity is present.
When all are blank, NovaDock derives the task name from Target as before. If
a running identity has no window that can be activated, NovaDock reports
that fact instead of launching a duplicate copy.

Workbench Tools entries and hot keys use the full Items catalogue, including
definitions currently removed from Organize. The extended fields are written
after the legacy icon/state fields, so older short configuration lines
continue to load unchanged.

LAUNCH= continues to launch AmigaDOS commands as in previous versions.
DRAWER= is a distinct type and does @{b}not@{ub} send its directory path to the
AmigaDOS Shell as an executable. On Workbench 3.5+ it uses
OpenWorkbenchObject() through workbench.library V44+. On older Workbench
systems it attempts C:WBRUN if that utility is installed. Classic 3.0/3.1
systems @{b}without@{ub} a compatible Workbench opener need a supported opener
installed before drawer activation can work; no filesystem changes are made
by an unsuccessful click.

@{b}Bundled icons@{ub}
-------------

  @{b}Icon       Path                 Suggested use@{ub}
  --------------------------------------------------------------
  Workbench  Icons/Workbench.iff  OS/tools/application shortcuts
  Drawer     Icons/Drawer.iff     Directory launchers
  Games      Icons/Games.iff      Game drawers or launchers
  Internet   Icons/Internet.iff   Network/browsers
  Tools      Icons/Tools.iff      Configuration/utilities

  * 32 x 32, @{b}IFF ILBM@{ub} 4 planes (16 indexed colors), transparent color 0.
  * Read directly on AmigaOS 3.0+ by the included portable IFF parser.
    Colors are translated to the nearest existing Workbench palette entries
    with FindColor(). NovaDock deliberately retains no palette pens, so
    applications such as Directory Opus 4 can open their default shared-pen
    custom screens after the dock has started.
  * The built-in IFF loader accepts raw or ByteRun1 compressed ILBM, up to
    64 x 64 and 4 planes, with transparency by index.
  * Other picture files are decoded through datatypes.library and the
    installed picture datatypes. For example, PNG artwork works when a PNG
    datatype is installed; an alpha/mask plane is retained when the datatype
    provides one. Artwork is aspect-fitted and translated to existing
    Workbench palette entries without asking picture.datatype to reserve
    shared pens.
  * Legacy Workbench .info is also supported using icon.library; omit .info
    from the path if preferred (GetDiskObject expects the base path). On
    icon.library V44+, NovaDock caches ColorIcons, NewIcons and classic
    planar icons as screen-mapped bitmaps. It honors generated masks and
    removes only dithered background pixels connected to the border of older
    icons; identical colours enclosed inside the artwork are preserved. The
    result is aspect-fitted to the dock tile. Older systems keep the planar
    renderer, with a textual fallback for unsupported or oversized icons.
  * NovaDock treats DataTypes as optional. Missing formats fall back to the
    appropriate Workbench application or drawer icon, so configurations
    remain usable on minimal installations.

@{b}Installation or upgrades@{ub}
------------------------

Execute build.amiga compiles the unified NovaDockPrefs as well as the dock.
Execute install.amiga copies that single Preferences tool and the five .iff
icons into NovaDock:Icons/. Existing user configuration is preserved,
including older two-field and three-field LAUNCH= entries. Add new launcher
entries via Preferences or edit the configuration file. On a fresh install,
two stock launcher tiles show bundled example icons.

@{b}Dock context menu and direct manipulation (NovaDock 0.1)@{ub}
--------------------------------------------------------

The runtime dock includes a small native Intuition popup for each item type.
Apps offer @{b}Launch@{ub}, @{b}Edit settings...@{ub}, and @{b}Remove@{ub}. Drawers also offer @{b}Popup
contents@{ub}, a browsable icon grid or list with sorting, nested drawers and
type-ahead (see DRAWER_POPUPS.md), which opens files or subdrawers without
first opening the Workbench drawer window. Changes are edited in the unified
NovaDockPrefs application.

A new setting determines normal left-click behavior:

    LEFT_CLICK=LAUNCH

  * LAUNCH: normal left click opens an app/drawer (selected in the bundled
    sample); @{b}right-click@{ub} opens its context popup with @{b}Launch@{ub} first.
  * MENU: normal left click opens the same popup instead.
  * NovaDockPrefs offers the same option as @{b}Click: Run / Menu@{ub}.
  * Plugins keep their old click behavior: one click triggers the plugin's
    click action; Shift+click changes graph vs meter.
  * An inactive dock accepts the first right-click: NovaDock observes the
    right-button-down event without consuming it, activates the dock, waits
    for the relative pointer position to settle, then opens the menu for
    that tile. The live input-device qualifier remains as a compatibility
    fallback. No left-click selection is needed.
  * Popup height follows the number of actions. Click outside a popup to
    close it; no dedicated Cancel row is shown.

@{b}Choose Unlock dock to rearrange:@{ub} NovaDock starts locked so an ordinary drop
cannot accidentally alter the dock. Dropping a Workbench object onto the
centre of an application tile is still available while locked: it opens that
object with the application and does not change the layout. Right-click a
dock gap and choose @{b}Unlock dock@{ub} for drag-based layout editing. Explicit
right-click @{b}Edit settings...@{ub} and @{b}Remove@{ub} commands work consistently for
launchers and plugins; launch-capable plugins also offer @{b}Launch@{ub}. These
commands work while locked because selecting one is already an unambiguous
edit request. The accent outline means Edit mode is active. Choose @{b}Add
item...@{ub}, then @{b}Spacer@{ub}, to append a persistent divider; it can then be dragged
into place, removed from its own menu, or dragged out just like another dock
item.

@{b}Drag an app or drawer from Workbench onto the dock:@{ub} The dock registers its
window with Workbench using AddAppWindowA() (available on AmigaOS 2.0+). The
drop message contains file/drawer locks. NovaDock copies and validates the
paths and type, then creates the matching application or directory entry. It
uses the object's own Workbench .info icon by default. For volume roots it
correctly resolves the special Disk.info name; if no private icon exists, it
uses Workbench's current system-default disk, drawer, or tool icon.
Volume-root tiles such as System: and Scratch: show a live used-space meter
by default. Every directory tile can use the same meter for the volume that
contains it: right-click the tile, choose @{b}Edit@{ub}, then switch @{b}Show space usage@{ub}
on or off. Ordinary subdirectories such as SYS:Utilities default to off,
while roots ending in : default to on. A disabled meter is not polled,
drawn, or added to the tooltip. Older configuration lines without an
explicit choice use those same root-on/subdirectory-off defaults; the final
extended field stores the per-drawer choice. Multiple dropped objects are
handled in drop-message order. The centre three fifths of an application
tile is its @{b}Open With@{ub} target. NovaDock launches one application instance per
dropped object, placing the action's configured arguments before the quoted
object path and respecting its working directory. ARexx actions, drawers,
plugins and spacers are not Open With targets. In Edit mode, the outer
fifths of a tile remain insertion zones; dropping there inserts before that
tile, while dropping on the surrounding dock background appends the objects.

NovaDock may be started from User-Startup before LoadWB has fully brought
the Workbench process online. If Workbench initially rejects the AppWindow,
NovaDock retries registration from its normal low-rate timer (or Intuition
ticks when timer.device is unavailable). No manual reload is required.

@{b}Rearrange existing dock items:@{ub} In Edit mode, hold the left button on any
tile and drag to another tile position, then release. The source item moves
to the destination position; it is never activated during a drag. An
insertion line shows the destination. Releasing over an empty dock gap is a
safe no-op; only releasing genuinely outside removes an item from the active
dock. Its definition remains available in Preferences. This works for
launchers, drawers, plugins, and spacers.

@{b}Persistence:@{ub} Direct rearranging, removal and Workbench drops update the
editable ENV:NovaDock/novadock.cfg, live-reload the dock and write the same
full configuration into ENVARC:NovaDock/novadock.cfg. Disabled plugins are
preserved because interactive changes operate on the @{i}full@{ui} configuration, not
the running dock's compacted list. If every tile cannot fit at the minimum
readable size, full-sized @{b}PREV@{ub} and @{b}NEXT@{ub} tiles page through the items. The
application supports at most 12 tiles in this release. A short status window
confirms accepted/rejected drops, moves, removals and lock changes.

@{b}Compatibility:@{ub} The native Workbench v36 AppWindow interface is used for
drops; no MUI/RTG is required. Classic .info images and 32x32 palette-based
ILBM images remain supported. Workbench v44+ can use OpenWorkbenchObject
when available, with Shell execution as a fallback for eligible programs. A
drawer opener (C:WBRUN) is still needed on some earlier OS3 configurations.

@{b}Testing:@{ub} Portable unit tests cover config movement, overflow geometry,
disabled plugin preservation, and serialization. Native 68k compilation and
the locked dock menu have also been exercised on Workbench.

@{b}Later 0.1: tooltips, Workbench drops, ARexx@{ub}
-------------------------------------------

See ARexx_AND_DROPS.md for drag-to-add/remove behavior, tooltips and control
commands.

@{b}Stack, quoting and running applications@{ub}
---------------------------------------

  * Every launcher, plugin and helper is started in a shell that first runs
    Stack, because a shell created by a Workbench-started dock would
    otherwise give commands only 4 KB. The stack is 16 KB, or the stack in
    the program's tool icon when that is larger.
  * Paths are quoted with AmigaDOS escaping (* becomes **, a quote *"), so
    names containing * or " work in working directories, drawer popups and
    plugin paths.
  * Running-application detection now also recognises programs started from
    a shell (including everything NovaDock launches) by their CLI command
    name, not only Workbench-started tasks. Click-to-front re-checks that
    the window still exists and brings its screen forward when the
    application uses its own screen.

Applications without configured launchers never create dock tiles. To browse
them without changing the dock layout, add the optional @{b}Running Apps@{ub} plugin;
see docs/TASKBAR.md.

@{b}Consistent artwork placement@{ub}

Normal-size launcher and launcher-style plugin artwork fits within 48x40
pixels (or the available tile area when smaller), preserving its aspect
ratio and never enlarging small sources. Normal and double-width tiles use
the same artwork limit. Icons share one vertical center above their bottom
label. This also applies to the real configured DOpus application icon. Tiny
tiles retain their compact artwork area and central placement.
@endnode

@node RESPONSIVE_SETTINGS "Responsive settings and diagnostics"
@toc Main
@{b}Responsive settings and diagnostics@{ub}
===================================

Moving, adding and removing dock items, switching the CPU/RAM view, and
changing folder view/sort update the dock immediately. Rapid edits share an
in-memory full configuration, including disabled plugins and their settings.
After about two seconds without another edit, the separate SaveConfig
program writes a snapshot to ENV: and ENVARC:. It runs at a lower priority
than the dock.

Only one writer runs at a time. Changes made during a write remain queued
for a later snapshot. A failed write keeps the latest changes in memory and
retries after roughly ten seconds; it never reports those changes as saved.

Preferences synchronizes queued edits before loading a session. Its live
configuration writes acquire a short dock writer lease, preventing overlap
with the background writer. Tile edits are refused briefly while Preferences
owns that lease. Saved configuration files retain the existing validation,
backup and known-good recovery handling. Manual external writes made during
a pending dock save do not participate in this coordination; use Preferences
or wait until Diagnostics reports no pending changes.

Quit flushes the latest edits before removing the dock's public ports. If a
save fails or takes longer than the shutdown wait, the dock stays running
and shows a message so the unsaved changes can be inspected or retried.
SaveConfig must stay beside the NovaDock executable; the installer copies it
there.

Diagnostics separates these operations:

  * @{b}Last update / Update paths@{ub}: whether the window was reused, rebuilt,
    skipped because nothing changed, or an update failed.
  * @{b}Update time@{ub}: configuration/layout application, excluding the later save.
  * @{b}Settings save@{ub}: saved, queued, writing in the background, or waiting to
    retry.
  * @{b}Save batches / Save failures@{ub}: completed snapshots, edits combined into
    batches, and failed attempts.
  * @{b}Save time / Last ENV write / Last ENVARC write@{ub}: the most recent writer's
    elapsed time and individual file results. A prior successful write does
    not imply that newer queued edits have already been saved.

Older docks are identified as lacking the additional telemetry instead of
showing a fabricated clean-save state.

@{b}Status dots@{ub}
-----------

Plugin process liveness is reported in Diagnostics, not as an automatic tile
dot. AmIRC publishes a confirmed IRC connection; Roadshow publishes its
stack state; Directory Opus publishes whether the application is resident.
Music uses its detected player's advertised controls. These marks follow the
running-app indicator setting. A traffic sampler does not claim an Internet
connection merely because its process or network driver is running.

Explicit notification badges, such as a Trash item count, remain available.
Third-party plugins can deliberately publish notification badges through the
existing negotiated badge API. No plugin wire ABI was changed.

@{b}Recovery and low-resource preset@{ub}
--------------------------------

Diagnostics offers @{b}Recover settings@{ub}. Choose ENV or ENVARC and then the
known-good or previous backup generation. NovaDock validates the selected
file before offering to load it into the Preferences session. Existing
session changes are replaced only after that confirmation. Use or Save
applies the recovered configuration through the normal settings workflow.

@{b}Low-resource preset@{ub} selects simple refresh, list-style drawer popups, meter
views in place of graphs, two-second CPU/RAM/Network polling, and disables
launcher scans and always-on-top. These changes stay in Preferences until
Use or Save. The preset is applied atomically; unavailable memory or
configuration capacity leaves the session unchanged. Plugin updates can be
less frequent.

@{b}IPC recovery@{ub} reports late replies reclaimed by the dock (saturating at
65,535). Existing per-plugin rows show update age, stale/offline state and
restart counts. Full native timing and input behavior still require testing
on the target Workbench setup.
@endnode

@node AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU "Auto-hide and the dock menu"
@toc Main
@{b}Auto-hide and the dock menu@{ub}
===========================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Auto-hide " link AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU 14}
  @{" Dock-wide menu " link AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU 42}
  @{" Highlighting " link AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU 107}
  @{" Keyboard control " link AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU 121}
  @{" Workbench smoke checklist " link AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU 147}
  @{" Showing and hiding on demand " link AUTOHIDE_AND_DOCK_MENU 184}

This is a @{b}source prototype@{ub}. Portable tests and native 68k compilation pass;
the dock has also been exercised on Workbench.

@{b}Auto-hide@{ub}
---------

In SYS:Prefs/NovaDockPrefs, set @{b}Hide@{ub} to @{b}On@{ub} and select @{b}Use@{ub} or @{b}Save@{ub}. Or edit
the same text file manually:

    AUTO_HIDE=YES
    HIDE_DELAY=3

AUTO_HIDE=NO is the backwards-compatible default. HIDE_DELAY is an integer
from @{b}1 to 60 seconds@{ub}, default @{b}3@{ub}. The dock checks the pointer five times per
second using one asynchronous timer.device request. The timer is not started
when auto-hide is off; selecting Hide=On while running starts it on live
reload.

When the pointer stays outside the dock for the configured time, the dock
contracts to a @{b}42×12 px horizontal tab@{ub}, or @{b}12×42 px vertical tab@{ub}, near the
configured screen edge. Pointing at the tab restores the dock. Clicking the
tab also restores it. Popup menus, drawer flyouts, the clock calendar,
tooltips, and active drag operations suspend hiding. Plugins continue
running while hidden but their tiles are not repainted until the dock is
visible.

The event code waits for IDCMP_CHANGEWINDOW before painting the changed
window size because ChangeWindowBox() is asynchronous. It does not rely on
IDCMP_INTUITICKS for background polling; Intuition only sends those to the
active window. If timer.device cannot be opened, NovaDock remains visible.

@{b}Dock-wide menu@{ub}
--------------

Right-click the background/gap of the dock to open the compact dock menu:

  * Unlock dock / Lock dock
  * Add item... (Application, Drawer / disk, installed Plugin, Spacer, or
    Command tile)
  * Preferences...
  * About NovaDock...
  * Quit NovaDock

Click outside any dock popup to dismiss it; a separate Cancel row is not
needed. Row count and row assignment are managed in Preferences, where the
result can be reviewed before Use or Save.

@{b}About NovaDock...@{ub} opens a small non-modal window with NovaDock's name,
version, short description, MIT license and public website. The same shared
information is available from the dedicated About section in Preferences.

NovaDock starts locked. Unlocking enters Edit mode, shown by an accent
frame, and enables direct Workbench drops, reordering and drag-out
removal/disable. Locking returns the dock to normal launch-only use. Brief
adjacent status windows confirm the mode and the outcome of every direct
edit. The Add Item window uses fixed-width type buttons and a separate field
area, so changing category never resizes or overlaps the controls. It shows
only the paths, arguments, appearance, or command options relevant to that
type. The @{b}Plugins@{ub} category lists installed manifest-backed plugins which are
not already enabled on the dock; selecting one shows its name and executable
and Use or Save activates it. Vertical docks remain one column. Adding a row
creates an empty row. In Edit mode, release a tile over another row's tile
or empty background to move it there. Row membership is saved, rows may
contain only one tile, and the frame width tracks the widest populated row.
Removing a row merges its tiles into the preceding row.

Right-clicking a tile immediately opens its own menu without selecting it
first. Apps retain Launch/Edit/Remove. Drawers add @{b}Popup contents@{ub}. The
Plugins use the same concise actions as launchers: @{b}Edit settings...@{ub} and
@{b}Remove@{ub}. A @{b}Launch@{ub} entry is shown only when the plugin has a genuine
application-launch action (currently DOpus, and Music when a default player
is configured). Clock calendars and meter display changes remain direct tile
actions. Spacers offer Edit spacer and Remove spacer. The spacer editor
controls the item's width, divider visibility, line thickness and
theme-aware colour. All configuration entry points open the same
NovaDockPrefs application.

Locking protects drag-reordering, drag-removal and structural background
actions. Explicit item-menu commands such as @{b}Remove@{ub}, @{b}Remove spacer@{ub} and
@{b}Remove@{ub} remains available while locked because choosing the command is itself
confirmation of the edit.

The @{b}Appearance...@{ub} page in NovaDockPrefs shows a live sample of launcher,
clock, CPU-meter and volume-meter tiles before applying a theme. Each theme
pack defines its own palette and default shape; the shape control can still
override that default. Themes use native solid colour roles only; graphic
dock backdrops are intentionally disabled. Optional picture datatypes remain
available for individual launcher artwork.

General also offers @{b}Refresh: Low mem / Smooth@{ub}. Low mem is the default and
uses Intuition's simple-refresh path, which is safest on classic planar
screens and leaves more display memory available to applications such as
Directory Opus 4. Smooth opts into a smart-refresh backing bitmap to reduce
expose redraws on systems with sufficient graphics memory. Use @{b}Use@{ub} for the
current session or @{b}Save@{ub} to make the choice permanent.

@{b}Highlighting@{ub}
------------

  * A launcher pressed with the left button gets a high-contrast accent
    border.
  * Edit mode draws an accent frame around the dock.
  * A dragged item draws an insertion line at its prospective drop position.
  * The hover background changes, and existing tooltips remain optional.

When the configured tiles do not fit at their minimum readable size, the
dock uses full-sized PREV/NEXT tiles to page through them. The controls
follow the dock orientation and do not replace or discard configuration
entries.

@{b}Keyboard control@{ub}
----------------

Set a @{b}Dock hot key@{ub} on the General page of NovaDock Preferences, for example
ctrl alt d (any Commodities hot key description; leave it empty for none).
Pressing it reveals the dock, makes it the active window and highlights a
tile. A cursor key does the same while the dock window is already active.

  @{b}Key              Action@{ub}
  ----------------------------------------------------------
  Cursor keys      Move to the previous or next visible tile
  Return or Space  Act on the tile, exactly like a click
  M                Open the tile's menu
  Esc              Leave keyboard control

In the tile's menu, cursor up and down choose an entry, Return or Enter
selects it, and Esc closes it. A drawer opened with Return continues with
the drawer popup's own keys (cursor keys, Return, Backspace, type-ahead).

Moving the mouse, or activating another window, hands control back to the
mouse. Auto-hide waits while keyboard control is active, and the selected
tile shows its tooltip after the usual delay. Only the tiles on the current
page are reached; use the PREV/NEXT tiles to change page. When keyboard
control is not active, Esc or Q in the active dock window still quits
NovaDock, as before.

@{b}Workbench smoke checklist@{ub}
-------------------------

  1. Build on OS3.0-3.2 with vbcc + NDK and start NovaDock. Confirm normal
     launchers and all nine plugins function with AUTO_HIDE=NO.
  2. Turn Hide on through NovaDockPrefs; verify live reload and that the
     setting is saved into the correct ENV/ENVARC file.
  3. On 640×256 Workbench, verify bottom/top tabs; on a tall-enough screen,
     verify left/right docks (nine vertical 68px tiles will not fit 256
     lines).
  4. Move the mouse to another @{b}active@{ub} Workbench window; wait the configured
     time and confirm the tab appears even though NovaDock is inactive.
  5. Move the pointer over the tab (without clicking), then click it to
     confirm both methods restore the dock.
  6. Right-click a blank dock area and several tile kinds; test the compact
     dock menu and contextual item menus without preselecting a tile.
  7. Drag an application tile over another tile: ensure the border indicates
     placement and that disabled plugin entries survive reordering/saving.
  8. Drag a launcher outside to delete it and a plugin outside to disable
     it; drop a Workbench icon into the dock to add it at the pointed
     position.
  9. Run MusicPlayer/Roadshow/Network updates while hidden and confirm they
     resume updating the correct tile on reveal.
  10. Disable auto-hide while hidden using Preferences/ARexx reload and
      confirm the dock reopens visibly rather than remaining a tab.
  11. Exit and restart repeatedly; watch for stuck Exec/timer.device/ARexx
      ports, memory leaks, broken refreshes, and orphan plugin processes.

@{b}Limitations:@{ub} no sliding animation yet; auto-hide is deliberately a small
static reveal tab to avoid CPU-heavy full-frame animation on 68000 systems.
The timer, refresh-mode and window-resize integrations remain untested on
physical hardware.

In this release right-clicking a plugin tile opens its own context menu,
including @{b}Plugin settings...@{ub}, rather than the dock-wide menu. Right-click
blank dock space for dock-wide actions.

@{b}Showing and hiding on demand@{ub}
----------------------------

Even with auto-hide off, the dock can be collapsed to its reveal tab and
brought back:

  * Commodities Exchange lists @{b}NovaDock@{ub} with working @{b}Show@{ub} and @{b}Hide@{ub} buttons
    (and Enable/Disable for its hot keys).
  * ARexx: SHOW, HIDE and TOGGLE.
  * Starting NovaDock a second time reveals and raises the running dock
    instead of failing.

A manually hidden dock reopens when the pointer touches its tab, as with
auto-hide.

@{b}Keep front@{ub} now skips the layer operation when nothing overlaps the dock, so
it no longer redraws needlessly five times a second.
@endnode

@node DRAWER_POPUPS "Drawer popups"
@toc Main
@{b}Drawer popups@{ub}
=============

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Layout " link DRAWER_POPUPS 12}
  @{" Using it " link DRAWER_POPUPS 33}
  @{" Defaults " link DRAWER_POPUPS 63}
  @{" Native testing checklist " link DRAWER_POPUPS 76}

Right-click a drawer or volume tile and choose @{b}Popup contents@{ub} to browse the
drawer without opening a Workbench window.

@{b}Layout@{ub}
------

    +-----------------------------------------+
    | <  Projects               Name  =       |   header
    |  [icon]   [icon]   [icon]   [icon]      |
    |  Apps     Docs     Games    readme      |   icon grid (or list)
    +-----------------------------------------+

  * @{b}Icon grid@{ub} shows each object's own classic Workbench icon, scaled down to
    fit when it is large. Colour 0 is transparent so icons sit on the theme
    colour. Objects without an icon get a drawer or file symbol. GlowIcons
    and NewIcons show their classic fallback image, because NovaDock never
    remaps the Workbench palette.
  * @{b}List@{ub} shows a symbol, the name and, for files, the size.
  * Drawers come first, then files sorted by @{b}Name@{ub}, @{b}Date@{ub} (newest first) or
    @{b}Size@{ub} (largest first).
  * Up to 64 objects are listed; a + after the title means the drawer holds
    more (open it in Workbench to see everything). A bar on the right edge
    shows the scroll position.

@{b}Using it@{ub}
--------

  @{b}Click a drawer@{ub}
      Shows its contents in the popup
  @{b}Click a file@{ub}
      Opens it through Workbench and closes the popup
  @{b}Shift- or Alt-click a drawer@{ub}
      Opens it in a Workbench window
  @{b}< in the header, Backspace@{ub}
      Up one level (never above the tile's drawer)
  @{b}Click the title@{ub}
      Opens the shown drawer in Workbench
  @{b}Click the sort name@{ub}
      Cycles Name, Date, Size
  @{b}Click = / #@{ub}
      Switches between list and icon grid
  @{b}Typing@{ub}
      Jumps to the first name starting with the typed letters; the same
      letter again steps to the next match; a pause of a second starts over
  @{b}Cursor keys@{ub}
      Move the selection (in the list, Left goes up and Right enters a
      drawer)
  @{b}Return / Shift-Return@{ub}
      Open the selection / open a drawer in Workbench
  @{b}Mouse wheel@{ub}
      Scrolls (NewMouse wheel events)
  @{b}Esc, right button, clicking elsewhere@{ub}
      Close the popup

@{b}Defaults@{ub}
--------

The view and sort order you pick in a popup are remembered, written to ENV:
straight away and to ENVARC: a few seconds later. They are stored as:

    FOLDER_VIEW=ICONS    (or LIST)
    FOLDER_SORT=NAME     (or DATE, SIZE)

Reading a drawer and its icons never raises "Please insert volume"
requesters. On floppy-based systems the list view is faster, because the
icon grid reads every object's .info file.

@{b}Native testing checklist@{ub}
------------------------

  1. Open a drawer popup on SYS:; check icons, scaling and the + marker in a
     large drawer such as SYS:C.
  2. Enter a sub-drawer, go up with < and Backspace, and check the popup
     never goes above the tile's drawer.
  3. Type the first letters of a name; press the same letter repeatedly.
  4. Switch view and sort, close, reopen, and restart NovaDock: the choice
     is kept.
  5. Open the popup on a floppy tile and remove the disk: no requester
     appears.
@endnode

@node REMOVABLE_MEDIA "Removable media"
@toc Main
@{b}Removable media@{ub}
===============

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Using the tiles " link REMOVABLE_MEDIA 14}
  @{" What counts as removable " link REMOVABLE_MEDIA 36}
  @{" How it works " link REMOVABLE_MEDIA 63}
  @{" Native testing checklist " link REMOVABLE_MEDIA 82}

When a floppy, PCMCIA/CF card, USB stick or mounted disk image is present,
NovaDock shows it as a temporary drawer tile at the end of the dock. The
tile disappears when the medium is removed. Nothing is written to the
configuration file.

@{b}Using the tiles@{ub}
---------------

  * @{b}Click@{ub} opens the volume, like any drawer tile.
  * @{b}Drop@{ub} files or drawers on the centre of the tile to copy them onto the
    disk (works while the dock is locked).
  * @{b}Right-click@{ub} offers:
    * @{b}Open@{ub} and @{b}Popup contents@{ub} - as for drawers.
    * @{b}Flush (safe to remove)@{ub} - inhibits and immediately re-enables the
      drive, which makes the filesystem write all pending data. The disk is
      then safe to remove until something writes to it again. Amiga drives
      have no motorised eject, so this replaces an "Eject" command.
    * @{b}Pin to dock@{ub} - adds a permanent drawer tile for the volume. The
      temporary tile then disappears, because a pinned volume is never shown
      twice.
  * The used-space meter is shown as for pinned volumes.
  * Temporary tiles cannot be dragged, reordered or removed; they always
    stay after the configured items. Pin a disk first to arrange it. New
    launchers dropped on the dock are inserted before the temporary tiles.

A status message reports @{b}Disk inserted@{ub} and @{b}Disk removed@{ub}.

@{b}What counts as removable@{ub}
------------------------

A volume qualifies when its DOS device is handled by one of these exec
devices:

    trackdisk.device   internal and external floppy drives (DF0:-DF3:)
    mfm.device         CrossDOS PC floppies (PC0:, PC1:)
    carddisk.device    PCMCIA memory cards on A600/A1200
    compactflash.device  CF cards in the PCMCIA slot
    usbscsi.device     Poseidon USB mass storage
    massstorage.device other USB mass-storage stacks
    diskimage.device   mounted ADF/HDF images

Override the list in novadock.cfg with a comma-separated replacement, for
example to include a CD-ROM on a separate controller:

    OPTION.MEDIA.DEVICES=trackdisk.device,usbscsi.device,cd.device

The volume holding SYS: is never shown, so a system that boots from a CF
card or USB stick does not get a tile for its own boot volume. At most four
temporary tiles are shown, and only while the 24-tile limit leaves room. If
the extra tiles would stop the dock fitting on screen, they are left out.

Switch the feature off with @{b}Media: Off@{ub} on the General page of Preferences,
or REMOVABLE_MEDIA=NO.

@{b}How it works@{ub}
------------

  * Detection reads only the DOS device and volume lists. NovaDock never
    calls Lock() on a removable volume to find out whether it is present, so
    an empty drive cannot raise "Please insert volume" or start a floppy
    motor.
  * A Commodities filter for disk-inserted and disk-removed input events
    starts a rescan within about half a second. A low-cost rescan every two
    seconds also covers drivers that mount volumes without those events (USB
    stacks, disk images).
  * Background work that touches volumes (icon loading and used-space
    meters, also for pinned volumes) runs with system requesters suppressed.
    A pinned floppy that is not in a drive therefore no longer brings up an
    insert-volume requester every few seconds; clicking it still asks for
    the disk as usual.
  * Changing the set of tiles reuses the normal configuration reload, so
    plugin state and graph history are kept.

@{b}Native testing checklist@{ub}
------------------------

  1. Insert and remove a floppy in DF0: and an external drive; check the
     tile appears within about two seconds and disappears on removal.
  2. Insert a disk whose volume is already pinned; no second tile appears.
  3. Use @{b}Flush@{ub}, then @{b}Pin to dock@{ub}, and check the result after a restart.
  4. Mount an ADF with diskimage.device and plug in a USB stick under
     Poseidon.
  5. Remove a pinned floppy and leave the dock running: no requester
     appears.
@endnode

@node TASKBAR "Running applications"
@toc Main
@{b}Running applications@{ub}
====================

NovaDock does not automatically add tiles for applications that are not part
of the configured dock. Starting MultiView, a Shell tool or another program
therefore never changes the dock's size, ordering or row layout.

The @{b}Apps@{ub} setting in @{b}Preferences > Appearance@{ub} controls only configured
launcher indicators:

  @{b}Off@{ub}
      Do not inspect configured launchers for running applications.
  @{b}Mark@{ub}
      Show a running mark and let a launcher click bring its application to
      front.

Older configurations may contain RUNNING_SECTION=YES from the retired
automatic taskbar mode. NovaDock accepts the line for compatibility, treats
it as NO, and writes NO on the next save.

@{b}Optional Running Apps plugin@{ub}
----------------------------

Add @{b}Running Apps@{ub} through the dock's @{b}Add item... > Plugins@{ub} page when an
application browser is useful:

  * The plugin occupies one stable, explicitly configured dock item.
  * Clicking it directly opens a native popup containing the currently open
    applications, sorted by window title.
  * Clicking a popup entry closes the popup and brings that application's
    window and screen to the front.
  * Workbench drawer windows, NovaDock and Preferences are omitted.
  * Multiple windows owned by one application task are represented by one
    entry.
  * Console windows without an application message port (including Shells)
    are listed individually and selecting one raises that exact window.
  * Closing the popup discards the list. The next click performs a fresh
    scan.

The popup list is collected when opened. A low-rate window count updates the
optional badge between openings. Popup opening runs in a separate helper,
independent of the resident badge/status poll; repeated clicks raise the one
existing popup. A stale inactive notification does not close an active
popup.

@{b}Native testing checklist@{ub}
------------------------

  1. Start an application that is not configured in the dock. Confirm that
     no new main-dock tile appears.
  2. Add and enable the Running Apps plugin, then click its tile.
  3. Confirm the new application's window title appears in the popup.
  4. Click the entry and confirm its screen and window move to the front.
  5. Close the application, reopen the popup and confirm the entry is gone.

Repeated clicks on Apps keep an already-open popup visible rather than
closing it again. The empty state has enough width to display its full
message. Apps lists running applications with eligible open windows, not
installed programs.

Type in the Apps popup to jump to a case-insensitive title match; Backspace
shortens the search. Arrow keys and Return retain their existing navigation
behavior. Search does not change the order of the running-window snapshot.
@endnode

@node THEMES "Themes"
@toc Main
@{b}Themes@{ub}
======

NovaDock keeps each bundled theme in its own directory below PROGDIR:Themes.
A pack contains one small text file:

    Themes/Copper/theme.cfg

@{b}Configuration@{ub}
-------------

The text file accepts these keys:

    NAME=Copper
    SHAPE=CHAMFER
    SIDE=BOTTOM
    POSITION=CENTER
    FONT=TOPAZ
    LABELS=ALWAYS
    SPACER_WIDTH=52
    SPACER_LINE=YES
    SPACER_LINE_COLOR=ACCENT
    SPACER_LINE_WIDTH=2
    BG=76,38,25
    TILE=76,38,25
    HOVER=111,58,34
    TEXT=255,230,199
    SUBTLE=205,154,112
    ACCENT=225,112,56
    BAR=92,51,37
    ACCENT2=54,176,165

Colours are decimal red, green and blue values from 0 through 255. SHAPE is
ROUNDED, SQUARE, CHAMFER or MINIMAL. BG fills the dock, TILE and HOVER
distinguish items, TEXT and SUBTLE control labels, ACCENT and ACCENT2
identify meters and graphs, and BAR is the unfilled meter colour. Rounded,
Square and Chamfer tiles also use HOVER for their upper/left edge and BAR
for their lower/right edge, producing a lightweight theme-aware bevel
without allocating extra palette pens. Minimal deliberately omits these
edges for a flat, undecorated style. BEVEL=YES draws a classic raised
Workbench frame around the whole dock (HOVER on the upper/left edge, SUBTLE
on the lower/right); the bundled Workbench theme sets it and every other
theme defaults to NO. Graphic dock backdrops are intentionally unsupported
for now. DataTypes-backed artwork still applies to individual launcher
icons.

SIDE (BOTTOM, TOP, LEFT or RIGHT), POSITION (START, CENTER or END), FONT
(WORKBENCH, TOPAZ or BOLD) and LABELS (ALWAYS, HOVER or HIDDEN) are also
part of a theme. They appear on the Appearance page and are applied when
that theme is selected. The user can still adjust them afterward. Older
custom theme files that omit one of these keys leave the current setting
unchanged.

SPACER_WIDTH is the theme's default spacer size from 20 through 140 pixels.
SPACER_LINE is YES or NO; SPACER_LINE_COLOR is SUBTLE, ACCENT, SECONDARY or
TEXT; and SPACER_LINE_WIDTH controls divider thickness from 1 through 8
pixels. Choosing a theme applies these defaults to the current spacers. Each
spacer can still be edited afterward to override them individually.

NovaDock has compiled-in fallback values, so a missing or partially edited
file does not make the dock unusable. Selecting a theme in Appearance loads
its declared placement, typography, label, shape and spacer treatment.
Changing a control or editing an individual spacer afterward is an explicit
override.

Selecting a theme updates only the sample inside Preferences. @{b}Test@{ub} applies
the complete preset temporarily and keeps Preferences open. @{b}Use@{ub} applies it
for the current session and exits; @{b}Save@{ub} applies it permanently and exits.
After a Test, closing Preferences restores the appearance that was active
when Preferences opened.

Refresh mode is deliberately not theme-bound. It is a machine resource
choice, not a visual style: Low mem remains safe when changing themes, while
Smooth is enabled only when the user explicitly selects it in General.

@{b}Bundled packs@{ub}
-------------

  * Mocha
  * Light
  * Workbench
  * Graphite
  * High Contrast
  * Copper
  * Ocean
  * Emerald
  * NeXTSTEP -- the intentional placement exception: a square, vertically
    centred right-edge dock inspired by the classic NeXT shelf, with labels
    always visible by default.
  * BeOS -- warm classic greys, yellow focus accents and chamfered tiles.
  * Nord -- a cool slate, ice-blue and muted green rounded preset.
  * Vapor -- a deep violet, magenta and cyan minimal preset with bold text.

All other bundled presets select a bottom-centred dock. NeXTSTEP selects
SIDE=RIGHT and POSITION=CENTER; placement can still be overridden after
selecting the preset.

The Appearance chooser is built dynamically. It shows the bundled presets
first, followed by every valid third-party theme found below PROGDIR:Themes,
sorted by its NAME. Adding a theme does not replace a bundled preset and
there is no fixed catalogue size; the practical limit is available Amiga
memory.

@{b}Third-party theme packages@{ub}
--------------------------

Each third-party theme lives in its own drawer. The drawer name is its
stable theme ID and may contain letters, digits, _ and - (up to 31
characters). The optional NAME= entry is the friendly label shown in
Preferences.

A recommended ZIP or LHA layout is:

    BlueCopper-1.0/
      README.txt
      Themes/
        BlueCopper/
          theme.cfg

README.txt should include the theme name, author, license, compatible
NovaDock version, theme ID, installation steps and uninstall steps. Theme
packs need no executable or install script.

To import a pack from the UI:

  1. Extract the package anywhere.
  2. Open NovaDock Preferences and choose @{b}Appearance@{ub}.
  3. Select @{b}Import...@{ub} and choose the pack's theme.cfg file.
  4. NovaDock validates the file, installs it as
     PROGDIR:Themes/<theme-id>/theme.cfg, and selects the new theme.
  5. Choose @{b}Test@{ub} to preview it on the dock, then @{b}Save@{ub} when satisfied.

Importing the same theme ID again updates that theme. NovaDock copies and
validates the new file before replacing the installed copy and restores the
previous file if installation fails.

For manual installation, copy the complete theme drawer below
NovaDock:Themes/, then reopen the Appearance page.

To uninstall an imported theme, select it in Appearance and choose
@{b}Import...@{ub}; NovaDock asks whether to import another theme or remove this one.
A theme the dock is using now, or that is saved, cannot be removed: select
and @{b}Use@{ub} or @{b}Save@{ub} another theme first. Removal deletes the theme's theme.cfg
and deletes its drawer only when nothing else is left in it. Bundled themes
cannot be removed.
@endnode

@node AREXX_AND_DROPS "ARexx, tooltips and drops"
@toc Main
@{b}ARexx, tooltips and drops@{ub}
=========================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Optional ARexx host " link AREXX_AND_DROPS 9}
  @{" Tooltip behavior " link AREXX_AND_DROPS 136}
  @{" Drag-and-drop behavior " link AREXX_AND_DROPS 154}
  @{" Native testing checklist " link AREXX_AND_DROPS 189}

@{b}Optional ARexx host@{ub}
-------------------

rexxsyslib.library is optional. When available, NovaDock registers a
standard ARexx host named @{b}NOVADOCK@{ub} (in addition to its existing, private
Exec IPC ports). The host does not change or replace the public plugin SDK.
An existing public port with that name is not taken over; NovaDock continues
without the ARexx interface if the name is occupied or the library is
missing.

Run this example in a .rexx script using C:RX (requires ARexx):

    /* NovaDock control demo */
    ADDRESS NOVADOCK
    'PING'
    SAY RESULT
    'LIST'
    SAY RESULT

Supported commands (case insensitive):

  @{b}PING@{ub}
      Returns PONG
  @{b}VERSION@{ub}
      Returns 0.1
  @{b}STATUS@{ub}
      Returns RUNNING and the number of visible tiles
  @{b}LIST@{ub}
      Returns semicolon-separated 1-based tile numbers and names for every
      tile
  @{b}LIST STEM tiles@{ub}
      Fills the ARexx stem TILES.: TILES.COUNT, and TILES.n.LABEL,
      TILES.n.KIND (LAUNCHER, DRAWER, GROUP, PLUGIN, SPACER, DISK or TASK),
      TILES.n.ID and TILES.n.VALUE (plugin tiles) for n = 1 to COUNT;
      returns the count. Labels may contain any character, including ;
  @{b}OPEN 2@{ub}
      Launches visible tile 2 (or invokes its configured action)
  @{b}OPEN Shell / OPEN "DOpus 4"@{ub}
      Launches the first tile whose label matches (case-insensitive, * and ?
      wildcards); RC 5 and NOT FOUND if none
  @{b}MOVE 3 5@{ub}
      Moves visible item 3 to position 5, saves configuration
  @{b}CLICK 4@{ub}
      Sends a click to plugin tile 4, exactly as a mouse click would
  @{b}REMOVE 2@{ub}
      Same as dragging tile 2 out: deletes a launcher, disables a plugin
  @{b}ADD "Work:Apps/Editor"@{ub}
      Appends an application or drawer launcher and saves
  @{b}SETVALUE id caption value [meter]@{ub}
      Updates a passive script tile (see below)
  @{b}SETBADGE id [text]@{ub}
      Shows TEXT (up to 7 characters, for example 3) as a badge on plugin
      tile ID; "*" shows a dot and no text removes it. Works on any plugin
      tile; a plugin that sets its own badges replaces it with its next
      change. RC 5 and NOT FOUND for an unknown ID
  @{b}SHOW / HIDE / TOGGLE@{ub}
      Reveals the dock, collapses it to the reveal tab, or switches; TOGGLE
      returns SHOWN or HIDDEN
  @{b}LOCK / UNLOCK@{ub}
      Leaves or enters Edit mode
  @{b}SUSPEND / RESUME@{ub}
      Closes all of the dock's windows so Workbench can reset its screen (a
      screen mode change), and reopens them; plugins keep running. The dock
      reopens by itself after 30 seconds, or when screennotify.library
      reports the new Workbench. RESUME returns RC 5 and PENDING if
      Workbench is not back yet
  @{b}RELOAD@{ub}
      Reloads text configuration from ENV:/ENVARC:/PROGDIR:
  @{b}PREFS@{ub}
      Requests SYS:Prefs/NovaDockPrefs
  @{b}QUIT@{ub}
      Gracefully requests dock shutdown
  @{b}HELP@{ub}
      Returns the supported command names

Arguments containing spaces are written in double quotes. ADD needs an
absolute path (one containing a colon); SETVALUE captions and values are
limited to 31 characters.

A stem avoids splitting the LIST string and works with any label:

    /* Print every tile */
    ADDRESS NOVADOCK
    OPTIONS RESULTS
    'LIST STEM tiles'
    DO i=1 TO tiles.count
      SAY i tiles.i.kind tiles.i.label tiles.i.value
    END

@{b}Script-driven tiles@{ub}

A plugin entry whose command starts with \@ is a @{i}passive tile@{ui}: NovaDock does
not start a process for it and the watchdog leaves it alone. Scripts fill it
with SETVALUE:

    PLUGIN=Mail|\@rexx|MAIL|METER|ON

    /* Show unread mail on the MAIL tile */
    ADDRESS NOVADOCK
    'SETVALUE MAIL "MAIL" "3 new" 30'

The optional meter is 0-100 and also feeds the tile's graph view. SETVALUE
is refused for tiles that belong to a real plugin process.

@{b}ARexx Launcher Actions@{ub}

Launchers in ARexx mode send their command asynchronously. NovaDock no
longer waits for the target application: a busy target cannot freeze the
dock or make plugins time out, and a target that is itself running a script
addressing NOVADOCK cannot deadlock it. A non-zero result is reported in the
status window when the reply arrives. On quit NovaDock waits up to about
five seconds for outstanding replies.

MOVE returns an error if its validated configuration change cannot be saved.
OPEN rejects plugin tiles; use the plugin's own mouse controls for their
plugin-specific actions.

MOVE works with any visible item, including plugins and spacers. Positions
refer to the currently visible tiles, not disabled plugin entries. Malformed
commands return a nonzero ARexx result code. The OPEN and MOVE operations
are local controls, not remote APIs; they have the same privileges as the
Workbench user. QUIT may terminate the dock and its plugin display.

The host returns strings only when the caller sets the normal ARexx result
request flag; it does not allocate results that the caller did not request.
This is a first implementation and must be verified with a native 68k build.

@{b}Tooltip behavior@{ub}
----------------

Set TOOLTIPS=YES (default) or TOOLTIPS=NO in novadock.cfg, or use the @{b}Tips:
On / Off@{ub} gadget in SYS:Prefs/NovaDockPrefs. A small borderless Workbench
popup appears over a tile after a short hover. The label uses the
application or plugin's configured name, with a length cap. Tooltips do not
read folders, launch programs, or allocate off-screen bitmaps. Tooltips
close on clicking, opening a launcher menu, losing focus or reloading the
dock.

@{b}Pointer tooltips@{ub}

Resting the pointer on a tile shows its ordinary tooltip when enabled in
Preferences > General > Tooltips. Selected-image substitution and zoom
windows have been removed. Old HOVER=OFF|IMAGE|ZOOM|BOTH configuration lines
are accepted for compatibility, ignored, and omitted on the next save.

@{b}Drag-and-drop behavior@{ub}
----------------------

  * NovaDock starts locked. Right-click the dock and choose @{b}Unlock dock@{ub} to
    enter Edit mode; its accent frame remains visible until it is locked
    again.
  * Drop a Workbench object on the centre of an application tile to open it
    with that application. This action works while locked, uses the
    launcher's stored arguments and working directory, and never changes the
    dock layout.
  * Drop files or drawers on the centre of a @{b}drawer or volume@{ub} tile to copy
    them there with C:Copy ... CLONE (drawers are copied as a new
    sub-drawer, ALL). This works while locked. A small window opens only if
    Copy reports an error. Whole volumes are never copied, and the Workbench
    window of the destination is not refreshed automatically.
  * In Edit mode, drag an application, data/project icon, or Workbench
    directory onto the background or outer edge of a tile: creates a
    launcher at that position, provided the 12-tile limit permits it. The
    centre of an application tile remains its Open With target.
  * In Edit mode, drag any dock tile onto another tile to reorder launchers,
    drawers, and plugins without activating the dragged item.
  * Drag a launcher or drawer genuinely outside the dock to remove it. A
    release over an empty dock gap is a no-op. Dragging a plugin outside
    disables its tile but preserves all plugin settings for re-enabling.
  * A transient status window explains accepted and rejected drops. On
    crowded screens PREV/NEXT tiles page through the dock without shrinking
    content below its readable minimum.

Creating launchers respects the stored text configuration and preserves
disabled plugins. Multi-object Workbench drops are inserted in their
supplied order. For Open With, each supplied object is launched separately
so one bad path does not discard the remaining objects. A launcher dropped
from Workbench uses Workbench or Shell startup in the same way as a launcher
created in the preferences editor.

@{b}Native testing checklist@{ub}
------------------------

  1. Boot Workbench 3.1 or 3.2 and compile with vbcc + NDK.
  2. Enable TOOLTIPS=YES; hover over a launcher, plugin and empty space.
  3. Test ADDRESS NOVADOCK followed by PING, LIST, OPEN, OPEN Shell, HIDE,
     SHOW, SETVALUE on an \@rexx tile and RELOAD.
  4. Drag a WBTOOL, drawer and project icon onto the dock; check distinct
     paths.
  5. Reorder a tile, drag a launcher out, and drag a plugin out; verify the
     item is removed from the active dock but remains available as a
     definition.
  6. Save a rearrangement; confirm both ENV: and ENVARC: configs persist.
  7. Quit, restart and run the ARexx commands again; check no stuck public
     ports.

The native 68k executable and locked dock menu have been exercised on
Workbench.
@endnode

@node EVENT_HOOKS "Event hooks"
@toc Main
@{b}Event hooks@{ub}
===========

NovaDock can run a command when something happens: a disk is inserted, the
network goes online, a plugin stops responding. Hooks are set in
novadock.cfg (Preferences keeps them when it saves), one per line:

    HOOK=EVENT|command

The dock runs the command in the background, with the event name and one
quoted detail appended, so one script can handle several events:

    HOOK=DISK_IN|Rexx:NovaDockEvents.rexx       runs  Rexx:NovaDockEvents.rexx DISK_IN "Work Disk"
    HOOK=NET_ONLINE|Run >NIL: SYS:C/SetDate     runs  Run >NIL: SYS:C/SetDate NET_ONLINE "NET"

  @{b}STARTUP@{ub}
      When: The dock has started and its plugins are running
      Detail: NovaDock version
  @{b}QUIT@{ub}
      When: The dock is quitting
      Detail: (empty)
  @{b}DISK_IN@{ub}
      When: A removable medium appeared (needs Media: On in Preferences)
      Detail: volume name
  @{b}DISK_OUT@{ub}
      When: A removable medium was removed (needs Media: On)
      Detail: volume name
  @{b}NET_ONLINE@{ub}
      When: The Network or Roadshow tile changed from offline to online
      Detail: tile ID
  @{b}NET_OFFLINE@{ub}
      When: The Network or Roadshow tile went offline
      Detail: tile ID
  @{b}PLUGIN_STALE@{ub}
      When: The watchdog restarts a plugin that stopped answering
      Detail: plugin ID
  @{b}SAVE_FAILED@{ub}
      When: Settings could not be saved
      Detail: (empty)

Up to eight hooks; an event may have several. The command is at most 95
characters. Network events report changes only: the first status after the
dock starts does not run a hook. A hook command starts like a launcher with
CLI start mode: use Run >NIL: or an ARexx script (RX, or a script file with
the s protection bit and a /* first line) for anything that should not open
a window.

An ARexx handler could look like this:

    /* Rexx:NovaDockEvents.rexx */
    PARSE ARG event detail
    detail = STRIP(detail,'B','"')
    SELECT
      WHEN event = 'DISK_IN' THEN ADDRESS COMMAND 'Say Inserted' detail
      WHEN event = 'NET_ONLINE' THEN ADDRESS COMMAND 'SetDate >NIL: NTP'
      OTHERWISE NOP
    END
@endnode

@node AMIRC_PLUGIN "AmIRC"
@toc Main
@{b}AmIRC@{ub}
=====

The optional AmIRC tile shows whether AmIRC is running and connected. It can
show connection status, your nickname, the current channel or the server
name. A numbered badge shows pending notification events when the supplied
hook is installed. When you are away, the caption changes to AMIRC AWAY. The
corner dot appears only after AmIRC confirms an IRC connection. It is
cleared while offline, disconnected, checking or unresponsive, and follows
the dock running-indicator setting. It does not indicate whether the bridge
process itself is running.

Right-click the dock background, choose @{b}Add@{ub}, and select @{b}AmIRC@{ub} from the
plugins. It is supplied disabled; the six default tiles stay unchanged.
Right-click its tile and choose @{b}Change settings...@{ub}:

  * @{b}AmIRC executable@{ub}: select your installed AmIRC program. This is needed to
    start AmIRC from the tile; an already running instance can be detected
    without setting the executable path.
  * @{b}ARexx port@{ub}: AUTO finds an AMIRC or numbered AMIRC.n public port,
    regardless of case. Set the exact port name to select a particular
    instance.
  * @{b}Display@{ub}: NICK (default), STATUS, CHANNEL, or SERVER.
  * @{b}Refresh ticks@{ub}: 100 by default; 50 ticks is one second. Normal NovaDock
    activity backoff also applies while idle or hidden.

Click the tile to start the selected program or restore AmIRC's window.
Workbench launch preserves AmIRC's normal icon/tooltype startup settings.
OFFLINE means there is no AmIRC port; DISCONNECTED means AmIRC is running
without a server connection. SET PROGRAM asks you to select its executable.
An unresponsive ARexx request displays NO RESPONSE; the dock continues
running.

The plugin uses AmIRC's local ISCONNECTED, GETAWAYSTATE, GETMYNICK,
GETCHANNEL, GETSERVERNAME and SHOW commands. The last command runs only when
you click the tile, or after a click has launched AmIRC. Launching AmIRC
uses AmIRC's existing startup preferences.

The bridge uses non-blocking ARexx exchanges with at most one outstanding
request. If a client never replies, the request remains owned by that
client; on plugin shutdown its reply target is made non-signalling and
retained rather than freed unsafely. This exceptional case retains one small
request/port and one ARexx library reference. Normal replies and shutdown
release all resources.

AmIRC is a separate application and is not bundled with NovaDock. The
official AmIRC download (https://www.amirc.org/download) includes its
AmigaGuide manual. Commands were checked against Documentation/AmIRC.doc in
the official AmigaOS 68020 package
(https://www.amirc.org/sites/default/files/amirc_39_68020.lha).

@{b}Numbered unread alerts@{ub}
----------------------

AmIRC 3.9 has no documented unread-counter query. NovaDock therefore counts
@{b}Private Msg@{ub} and @{b}Highlite@{ub} event notifications received while AmIRC is not
active. This is an unread @{b}event@{ub} count, not an exact per-channel message
counter. If AmIRC fires both events for one message, BOTH mode counts both;
choose PRIVATE or HIGHLIGHT if you prefer one source. No message contents or
IRC traffic are forwarded.

The installer places Rexx/NovaDockNotify.amirx in the NovaDock drawer. To
connect AmIRC once:

  1. Copy that script into your existing AmIRC Rexx drawer.
  2. In AmIRC's @{b}Settings -> Events@{ub}, set the @{b}Private Msg@{ub} Rexx command to
     NovaDockNotify.amirx PRIVATE.
  3. Set the @{b}Highlite@{ub} Rexx command to NovaDockNotify.amirx HIGHLIGHT.
  4. Save AmIRC's settings. Highlight matching uses AmIRC's existing Hilite
     list.

Keep any existing event scripts by calling this script from them instead of
replacing them. The hook quietly does nothing if the NovaDock AmIRC plugin
is not running. It uses the originating AmIRC port, so another client cannot
add to a tile pinned to a different port.

The badge shows 1, 2, etc., capped visually at 999+. It takes priority over
the connected dot and remains visible if you disable running-app dots.
Returning to an AmIRC window clears the count by default. A successful click
on the tile (AmIRC acknowledges SHOW) also clears it. A failed SHOW keeps
it. Switching to a different AmIRC instance or closing it resets the count.

@{b}Notification events@{ub} selects BOTH (default), PRIVATE, HIGHLIGHT or OFF. @{b}Clear
alerts when AmIRC is active@{ub} defaults to YES; set NO to acknowledge only
through the dock tile. Unread event counts are session-local, not saved.
@endnode

@node BITSKIM_PLUGIN "BitSkim"
@toc Main
@{b}BitSkim@{ub}
=======

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Add and use " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 16}
  @{" Send a file " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 31}
  @{" Results and details " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 54}
  @{" Right-click controls " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 75}
  @{" Settings and compatibility " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 90}
  @{" Artwork " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 120}
  @{" Tile layout " link BITSKIM_PLUGIN 127}

An optional NovaDock tile for the separately installed Amiga BitSkim client.
The updated BitSkim 0.1 source exposes ARexx protocol 2; version discovery
uses CAPABILITIES, not the application's unchanged 0.1 VERSION string.

@{b}Add and use@{ub}
-----------

Right-click the dock background, choose Add item, then Plugins and BitSkim.
Set @{b}BitSkim executable@{ub} if it is not at SYS:Utilities/BitSkim/BitSkim. The
existing default dock layout remains unchanged. Restart NovaDock once after
updating to load its enhanced context menu and file-drop handling.

Click to start BitSkim or restore its window, and acknowledge the latest
result alert. Idle tiles use BitSkim's icon. Transfers show SEND/RECV,
percentage and recent payload speed (K/s uses 1024 bytes). A zero rate means
no measured payload activity, not proof of a failed transfer. Pending
incoming offers show INCOMING FILE and a ! badge; outgoing offers show WAIT
ACCEPT.

@{b}Send a file@{ub}
-----------

Choose @{b}Send file...@{ub} from the tile's context menu, or drop one regular file
onto the tile. The helper opens BitSkim, then a centered recipient list
showing computer name, platform and address. Select a computer and press
@{b}Send@{ub}. Refresh can collect devices that appear after discovery resumes. No
file is sent until Send is pressed. Cancel closes the picker without
sending.

BitSkim must be running for Send and Details. Click its tile first if it is
offline. SHOW resumes discovery because iconified BitSkim clears its peer
list and pauses searches. The helper uses stable peer IDs and SENDID; it
never falls back to another peer, an old list index or a manual address. A
disappeared or ambiguous recipient is rejected by BitSkim. Incoming
acceptance remains in BitSkim's normal requester; the dock never accepts
automatically.

One regular file, at most 2,147,483,647 bytes, is supported. Drawers,
multi-file drops and batch queues are rejected. Filenames must also satisfy
BitSkim's portable filename rules. Paths with spaces and literal quotes are
encoded for BitSkim's command parser. A busy transfer rejects a second send.

@{b}Results and details@{ub}
-------------------

Protocol-2 clients retain the last terminal result. The tile shows SENT,
RECEIVED, TRANSFER FAILED, CANCELLED or DECLINED until acknowledged by
clicking it, or until a new transfer becomes active. A new successful result
gets a 1 badge; failure, cancellation and decline get !. These represent the
latest unacknowledged result, not a multi-transfer unread queue. Incoming
offers take priority while active. Badge OFF suppresses badges.

Open BitSkim to inspect filenames, computer names and error details, or to
change its application preferences. These controls stay in BitSkim itself.

The plugin negotiates capabilities and polls numeric STATUS plus
session-aware EVENTDETAILS. It rereads LAST when events advance or history
is lost and deduplicates results by qualified transfer ID. On application
restart or event generation change it resynchronizes; an already-retained
result at initial attachment is not announced as new. Rapid events can
exceed the 32-entry history: LAST is a single retained result, so
intermediate outcomes cannot be reconstructed and are not invented.

@{b}Right-click controls@{ub}
--------------------

  * Open BitSkim: start or restore the application.
  * Send file: choose one file and a recipient.
  * Toggle receiving: enable or disable receiving in the running
    application.
  * Cancel transfer: ask BitSkim to cancel the current transfer.
  * Edit settings: configure this dock plugin.
  * Remove: remove the tile.

Other controls require BitSkim to be running. Errors from rejected actions
use BitSkim's ERROR diagnostic when available. That diagnostic slot is
host-global, so the return code and transfer ID/result remain authoritative.

@{b}Settings and compatibility@{ub}
--------------------------

PROGRAM is the executable. PORT=AUTO discovers BITSKIM or BITSKIM.n; an
explicit port selects one copy. POLL_TICKS=50 is the initial refresh
interval (50 ticks per second), with NovaDock's normal idle backoff.
BADGE=PEERS shows nearby-device counts while idle; STATUS shows receiving
status instead; OFF hides badges. A running, receiving client with no peers
shows a dot. Offline and unresponsive clients show no dot.

Older clients returning RC 10 to CAPABILITIES retain the original numeric
status, progress and launch/restore/management controls. The new send helper
explains that protocol 2 is required; no legacy SEND fallback is used.

On the tested rexxsyslib 47.2 setup, CreateRexxMsg omitted its REXX marker,
causing BitSkim to return messages without handling them. The plugin now
supplies that marker only when missing and retains it until the reply (or
with an abandoned request). This fixes STATUS FAILED without replacing
BitSkim or the system library.

Each requester keeps at most one asynchronous ARexx message in flight. After
five seconds without a reply, the tile shows WAIT BITSKIM and keeps
answering dock IPC. It resumes automatically when the pending reply returns;
a valid status also clears a previous STATUS FAILED notice immediately. A
separate helper process owns pickers/requesters, so it does not block the
dock. A timed-out live message is not freed beneath BitSkim. Native BitSkim
accept/save/error requesters may delay ARexx replies until dismissed: a
timeout does not mean a transfer failed. The plugin opens no network sockets
and does not hold bsdsocket.library open during Roadshow shutdown.

@{b}Artwork@{ub}
-------

Plugins/BitSkim.info is from the supplied BitSkim source, copyright 2026
BitSkim contributors, under MIT. See BitSkim-icon-LICENSE.txt for its
notice.

@{b}Tile layout@{ub}
-----------

Active transfers use the same centered heading row as CPU/RAM, with SEND or
RECV and a percentage above the meter. Hover over the tile for transfer
speed and its full current status. Idle tiles keep the BitSkim icon and
bottom title.

BitSkim does not support graph mode. Clicking (including Shift-click) opens
or restores BitSkim; old saved GRAPH settings load as progress-meter mode.

If a pending request loses its registered destination (or that port changes
owner), the resident can reconnect using a fresh reply port. It never
resends an already-issued action. Old messages and reply targets remain
valid for late replies, which are discarded and reclaimed. At most four
lost-host requests are retained; further recovery waits for one to return or
for a manual plugin restart. Merely waiting in an accept/save dialog does
not trigger reconnection.
@endnode

@node NETWORK_PLUGIN "Network"
@toc Main
@{b}Network@{ub}
=======

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Tile and controls " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 20}
  @{" Setup " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 48}
  @{" Existing installations " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 106}
  @{" Network Details " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 123}
  @{" Waiting for shutdown " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 164}
  @{" Quiet operation and debug logging " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 206}
  @{" Saved display view " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 230}
  @{" Tile layout " link NETWORK_PLUGIN 243}

Network combines Roadshow control and the packet-activity sampler in one
tile. It is optional; add @{b}Network@{ub} from the dock's Add menu. The first
provider is Roadshow. Stack selection is AUTO or ROADSHOW; the
provider/control boundary is separate from sampling so another stack can be
added without another dock tile. Other stack providers are not implemented
yet.

@{b}Tile and controls@{ub}
-----------------

  * @{b}Offline:@{ub} a pre-generated network icon with NETWORK at the bottom, in the
    same style as Apps and Music.
  * @{b}Online:@{ub} two separate @{b}IN@{ub} and @{b}OUT@{ub} packet-activity meters by default.
    Incoming traffic uses the first accent color; outgoing traffic uses the
    second. Both bars use the same configured PPS scale. The value above
    them is IN/OUT p/s. Click the online tile to switch between meter and
    graph, like CPU/RAM.
  * @{b}Right-click -> Online:@{ub} start the selected Roadshow interface with
    C:AddNetInterface, including its normal DHCP configuration.
  * @{b}Right-click -> Offline:@{ub} first take the selected interface offline with
    C:ConfigureNetInterface <interface> OFFLINE QUIET, then stop all
    Roadshow interfaces through C:NetShutdown TIMEOUT=5 QUIET. This is a
    stack-wide shutdown, not merely hiding the tile.
  * @{b}Edit settings:@{ub} choose the network interface, view, stack and meter
    scale. Advanced contains optional sampling overrides. Left-clicking an
    offline tile does nothing; settings remain in its right-click menu.

Online means Roadshow reports operational interfaces; it does not promise
Internet reachability. Status polling first checks whether bsdsocket.library
is already resident, so it does not reopen the stack after a shutdown.

Control and status commands run in a separate worker. The plugin continues
servicing the dock while DHCP or shutdown takes time. One command runs at a
time; a later action is queued. Polling never initiates Online or Offline.

@{b}Setup@{ub}
-----

@{b}Interface = AUTO@{ub} prefers an active, non-loopback IPv4 interface reported by
Roadshow. When none is active, a detected A2065 board with its installed
driver and matching DEVS or Storage profile is preferred. Otherwise it picks
the first usable file, sorted by name, from DEVS:NetInterfaces for
connection startup. Choose a specific interface if needed. A matching file
is read for DEVICE and UNIT; it is never changed.

The @{b}Select@{ub} button beside Network interface opens a dedicated connection
profile list. It combines DEVS:NetInterfaces and SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces,
sorts names and removes duplicates (the DEVS profile takes priority). Each
row shows the profile and its driver, for example @{b}A2065 - a2065.device
[Template]@{ub}. Only profiles whose driver file is installed are offered; bare
driver names are checked in DEVS:Networks. Template means a profile from
Storage, not a storage adapter. Installed drivers do not prove that the
physical adapter is present. AUTO remains available. Double-click a row or
choose Select to put its profile name into settings; Cancel leaves it
unchanged. Settings Use/Save then applies it. No files need to be copied
from Storage for this to work. Online rereads the saved selection in its
worker and uses the exact profile path; it cannot fall back to another
profile if the selected file is missing.

The main selector never browses raw .device files. DEVS:Networks contains
drivers, while the profile also supplies unit and DHCP/IP configuration.
That driver-only drawer is available under Advanced for sampling overrides.
Only readable, valid profile files are listed; icon sidecars and driver
filenames are excluded. The current name is highlighted when present, and
names can still be typed in the settings field.

Driver and unit are derived automatically from the selected profile or the
live interface's Device name and Device unit. No separate device choice is
needed for normal operation. Selecting or cancelling a profile never starts
the network or modifies its profile file.

@{b}Advanced...@{ub} exposes @{b}Override sampling driver@{ub}, @{b}Sampling driver@{ub} and @{b}Sampling
unit@{ub}. The override switch defaults to NO. Enable it only for an unusual
sampling setup; its driver/unit fields are disabled otherwise. The advanced
driver Select button opens DEVS:Networks, filtered to .device files. This
override affects traffic sampling only, never the connection profile.
Returning to automatic mode clears the override on the next settings save.
Older device values cannot silently override automatic selection: they are
cleared in the loaded configuration unless the advanced override is
explicitly enabled. Changing the interface or sampling source also forgets
the previous resolved source so an earlier driver selection cannot be reused
accidentally.

@{b}Meter scale PPS@{ub} is the full-scale packets/second value, 250 by default.
Meter and graph channels are RX and TX. State messages clear the meters
rather than leaving the last traffic sample visible. These are packet rates,
not bytes/second or a bandwidth percentage. Driver counter resets and 32-bit
wraps are handled by the existing rate calculator.

If Roadshow is online but the driver cannot supply statistics, the tile
reports NO STATS rather than inventing traffic. Check the interface/device
settings.

@{b}Existing installations@{ub}
----------------------

An enabled legacy Roadshow tile becomes Network in the same position. If
both Network and Roadshow were enabled, Network is retained and the
duplicate is removed. Legacy Roadshow settings remain available as fallback.
The standalone Roadshow executable and its alias are no longer shipped. Add
Item and the plugin catalogue offer Network.

The user's saved file is not reset. The upgraded layout is persisted with
the next ordinary settings save. The six default active tiles remain
unchanged.

The Roadshow commands and their arguments were checked against its installed
GetNetStatus, AddNetInterface and NetShutdown reference manuals. No Roadshow
binaries are bundled with NovaDock.

@{b}Network Details@{ub}
---------------

Right-click Network and choose @{b}Details...@{ub} for a centered, scrollable
read-only window. It shows the selected stack, interface, SANA-II
device/unit and connection timeout. When Roadshow is running, it also shows
live interface IP configuration, routes/gateway and DNS servers using
C:ShowNetStatus.

Use @{b}Refresh@{ub} to reread the configuration and live values. Opening Details
while offline does not start the stack; unavailable live values are
explained in the window. The window runs independently of the dock and
traffic sampler. Opening Details again brings the existing window forward.

ShowNetStatus arguments were checked against its installed Roadshow
reference manual. No NAMES option is used, so the query does not wait for
reverse DNS.

The context menu offers Online while offline, and Offline while online.
Failed explicit state changes open an independent error requester; the dock
continues running. On larger Workbench screens all seven normal settings are
shown together; Advanced adds three sampling controls, with scrolling
reserved for screens too small to fit them.

If ShowNetStatus reports an unsupported status-query method, Details
explains that the active socket library cannot be queried through Roadshow.
Networking provided by another TCP/IP stack may not support Roadshow status
or control commands. Use the controls supplied with that stack.

AUTO first selects an active, non-loopback IPv4 interface reported by
ShowNetStatus, including wrapped status-table rows. It uses a matching
DEVS:NetInterfaces file for device/unit metadata. If the active interface
has no matching file, it reads the live interface's reported Device name and
Device unit for traffic sampling. If these cannot be read, it asks for an
explicit SANA-II device rather than borrowing an unrelated interface's
device. With no active interface, AUTO falls back to a configured interface
file for connection startup. Explicit selections and explicitly enabled
advanced sampling overrides are preserved. Active-interface discovery is
cached for 30 seconds during passive polling and refreshed for control
actions, settings reloads and Details.

@{b}Waiting for shutdown@{ub}
--------------------

Offline closes NovaDock's sampling request before starting NetShutdown. It
uses the last resolved interface, without new status queries. The worker
first takes that interface offline using ConfigureNetInterface, then
performs the full stack-wide NetShutdown. If the preparatory command fails,
full shutdown is not started and an error explains the failed step. This
sequence completed on the bridged A2065 test where direct NetShutdown hung
even without NovaDock running. It does not establish a general fault in
every Roadshow installation. A normal transition displays STOPPING. If the
command has not replied after 15 seconds, NovaDock opens an explanation and
displays WAIT STACK while its single background worker waits. A Roadshow
warning that shutdown remains pending is also explained. Close network
clients which may be holding sockets open. No second control job is started
while one is still running; the dock and other plugins remain active.

During pending shutdown the sampler, periodic status queries and Details
refresh queries are paused. NovaDock checks for the socket library to leave
without reopening it. Once shutdown completes, the tile becomes offline.
Commands are not forcibly killed because Roadshow shutdown cannot be
cancelled. Online jobs have the configured connection timeout plus a
15-second allowance. A driver request that cannot be drained prevents
shutdown from being started and produces a separate explanation instead of
releasing live I/O memory.

For an AUTO connection, NovaDock remembers the last active interface within
the running plugin so reconnecting targets that interface. If its file is
not installed in DEVS:NetInterfaces, a matching SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces
file can be used, as with the standard A2065 test template. Configuration
files are not created or changed automatically.

A2065 detection reads its expansion-board identity without initializing
unrelated network drivers. This lets AUTO start the A2065 Storage template
when its interface is inactive instead of trying an unrelated network
profile. The runtime metadata parser accepts both Device unit and Device
unit number.

After a manual binary update, quit and restart NovaDock. The private worker
protocol is version-checked, and parent/worker binaries must be from the
same build; files copied over an older resident process do not replace it.

@{b}Quiet operation and debug logging@{ub}
---------------------------------

@{b}Debug log@{ub} defaults to @{b}NO@{ub}. Before an explicit Online or Offline action,
NovaDock starts Roadshow's C:NetLogViewer CX_POPUP=NO if it is not already
running. Roadshow's separate log messages are captured in the hidden viewer,
so routine connection and shutdown notifications do not open its automatic
console. Command output remains quiet too. A viewer already running is
reused without relaunching it (relaunching would bring its window forward).

Choose @{b}Debug log = YES@{ub} to show the viewer for the next Online/Offline
action. Roadshow's own Shift+Alt+F8 hotkey or Commodities Exchange can also
show the retained messages. NovaDock does not change interface debug flags
or Roadshow startup files. An already-open log window is left available;
turning Debug log off prevents subsequent operations from opening it
automatically. Roadshow closes its viewer during full-stack shutdown, so the
next Online operation starts a new hidden viewer when debug logging is off.

NetLogViewer must be installed with Roadshow. If capture cannot be started,
NovaDock reports that error and leaves the requested operation unstarted,
rather than silently producing an unwanted console. Explicit connection or
shutdown failures still use NovaDock's normal error requester. Passive
polling and opening Settings never start the viewer or the network stack.

@{b}Saved display view@{ub}
------------------

@{b}Default view@{ub} in Network preferences offers METER and GRAPH, defaulting to
METER. Using or saving the preference applies the view to the tile. Clicking
an online tile switches view and saves the same preference in the
background, so preferences and the tile stay in sync. View-only changes
redraw the dock; they do not reload the Network sampler or change the
network connection. Existing configurations retain their saved PLUGIN view
until a VIEW preference is explicitly written. Offline left-clicks
(including keyboard activation) perform no action; right-click still exposes
Online, Details and settings.

@{b}Tile layout@{ub}
-----------

Online tiles align the NETWORK heading with CPU/RAM and show IN/OUT meters
or a two-channel graph. Packet rates and control/sampling status are
available in the hover tooltip. Offline tiles keep the network icon and
bottom title.
@endnode

@node CLIPBOARD_PLUGIN "Clipboard History"
@toc Main
@{b}Clipboard History@{ub}
=================

Right-click the dock background, choose @{b}Add -> Plugins -> Clippy@{ub}. It is
optional and does not change the six default tiles or an existing layout.
The tile uses a pre-generated clipboard icon and a bottom label, matching
Apps. An empty history reports EMPTY instead of treating absent clipboard
data as an error.

Click the tile to open a @{b}centered, themed quick picker@{ub} like Apps. Each card
previews a stored text clip. Click a card or use the arrow keys and Return
to copy it, then paste with Amiga-V in the target application. Escape or a
click outside closes it. A larger history is paged; use the footer's
left/right halves or the arrow keys at a page boundary. The picker takes a
stable memory snapshot so new clipboard activity cannot change the text
underneath your selection.

Right-click the tile and choose @{b}Manage history...@{ub} for the detailed list.
Select a row and use @{b}Copy@{ub}, or use its management controls:

  * @{b}Delete@{ub} removes the selected history entry.
  * @{b}Clear@{ub} removes the stored history, including a capture already in
    progress.
  * @{b}Pause / Resume@{ub} controls capture. Pausing retains existing entries.
  * @{b}Close@{ub} or Escape closes the popup.

Tile settings set the history limit (1-32, default 10) and its optional
numbered count badge. Identical clips are deduplicated and moved to the
front. History is kept only in the plugin's memory and is cleared when it
exits. There is no history file, network access or automatic clipboard
logging to disk. Standard AmigaOS clipboard storage is independent of this
plugin.

This version captures FTXT text from clipboard unit 0. It accepts multiple
CHRS chunks and skips other chunk types. Binary/image clips are ignored.
Entries larger than 8192 bytes, raw forms larger than 64 KiB, malformed
forms and embedded NULs are skipped whole, never silently truncated. Text
retains its original Amiga character bytes and line breaks.

A clipboard owner can post data lazily. Transfers are asynchronous and the
plugin continues answering NovaDock while waiting. @{b}CLIPBOARD BUSY@{ub} means a
transfer has not completed; another Copy is refused until it finishes. After
the dock closes, an outstanding transfer finishes quietly before its process
exits; this releases the system clipboard correctly even if the clipboard
owner supplies its data late. The list is intentionally bounded and reads at
most once per second when idle.

Deleting or clearing history does not clear the system clipboard. Pause
capture before copying text you do not want retained in the history.

@{b}Search and pinned snippets@{ub}

The management window has a case-insensitive search field. Enter text and
press Return to filter the history; Copy, Delete and Pin act on the
displayed selection. Pinned entries have a * marker and survive automatic
eviction by new clipboard text. The existing total history limit still
applies. If every entry is pinned, new text is not added until an entry is
unpinned or deleted. Explicit Clear/Delete and reducing the configured limit
can remove pinned entries. Pins and clipboard contents remain session-local.
@endnode

@node ARCHIVE_PLUGIN "Archive Extractor"
@toc Main
@{b}Archive Extractor@{ub}
=================

Archive Extractor is a bundled drop target for common Amiga archive formats.
It uses unpacker commands already installed in C:; NovaDock does not bundle
or replace those tools.

@{b}Use@{ub}
---

  1. Add or enable @{b}Extract@{ub} in Preferences > Organize. It is enabled in a
     fresh default configuration and offered in @{b}Add item > Plugins@{ub} after
     upgrading an older configuration.
  2. Drag an archive's Workbench icon onto the Extract tile. This works
     while the dock is locked.
  3. NovaDock creates a drawer beside the archive, using the archive
     filename without its final extension, and extracts into that drawer.

Archive Extractor is deliberately drop-only. An ordinary left click does
nothing; it cannot start an empty extraction or change the tile's display.

For example, dropping Work:Downloads/Demo.lha creates Work:Downloads/Demo/.
The tile reports EXTRACTING, then DONE or a short error status. A separate
@{b}NovaDock Archive Extractor@{ub} Shell window displays the unpacker's live,
file-by-file output and any prompts. None of the supported extractors are
started in quiet mode, so the archive contents remain visible as they are
unpacked. When it finishes, the window shows the result and remains open
until RETURN is pressed or its close gadget is clicked. Extraction runs in a
separate helper process, so the dock and its other plugins keep responding
while the window is open. After a successful extraction, the helper notifies
Workbench that the destination drawer was added; an open window for the
archive's parent directory refreshes automatically to show it.

@{b}Formats and commands@{ub}
--------------------

  @{b}Archive           Commands searched in C:@{ub}
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ZIP               UnZip, unzip
  LHA, LZH          LhA, lha
  RAR               UnRAR, unrar, RAR, rar
  ARJ               UnARJ, unarj, ARJ, arj
  LZX               LZX, lzx, UnLZX, unlzx
  TAR               tar
  TAR.GZ, TGZ       tar (GNU tar with -z; needs gzip in the path)
  GZ (single file)  gzip

The first matching command for that format is used. A format remains
unavailable until one of its commands is installed. Right-click the tile and
choose @{b}Edit settings...@{ub} to change only its lightweight status refresh
interval.

@{b}Safety and retry behaviour@{ub}
--------------------------

  * The destination drawer must not already exist. This avoids silently
    mixing an archive with old contents or prompting from a background
    command.
  * Paths containing shell control characters, wildcards or quotes are
    rejected.
  * Drawers and unknown extensions cannot be dropped on this tile.
  * A failed extraction removes the new drawer when it is still empty. If
    the unpacker had already written files, the extraction window asks
    whether to delete the partial drawer (C:Delete ... ALL) or keep it for
    inspection.
  * Every extraction writes its own T:NovaDockArchive-<id>.status file, so
    several archives can be dropped at once; the tile shows EXTRACTING 2
    while two are running and then the latest result.
  * Extractor shells receive a 16 KB stack.
  * Extractors receive an interactive CON: window for both input and output.
    This exposes their normal progress and error text and permits input when
    an installed tool requests it. NovaDock waits only inside the
    short-lived extraction helper, never inside the dock process.

NovaDock constructs these command forms:

    UnZip -o archive -d destination
    LhA x archive destination/
    UnRAR x -o+ archive destination/
    ARJ x -y archive destination/
    CD destination + UnARJ x archive
    LZX x archive destination/
    CD destination + UnLZX -x archive
    CD destination + tar -xvf archive        (or -xzvf for .tar.gz/.tgz)
    gzip >destination/name -dc archive.gz

For .tar.gz the destination drawer drops both extensions (src-1.0.tar.gz
becomes src-1.0/). A single .gz file is decompressed into a drawer of its
own name without the .gz.

Command paths and file paths are quoted by the implementation. The portable
host tests cover case-insensitive recognition, destination naming,
unsafe-path rejection and command construction.
@endnode

@node COMMAND_TILE "Command tiles"
@toc Main
@{b}Command tiles@{ub}
=============

A command tile runs any AmigaDOS command at a set interval and shows one
line of its output, optionally as a meter. It is a bundled plugin
(Plugins/Command); every tile is a separate instance with its own settings.

@{b}Adding one@{ub}
----------

  * Right-click the dock background, choose @{b}Add item...@{ub}, select @{b}Application@{ub},
    then choose @{b}Command tile@{ub} from its Type selector. Enter its command,
    refresh interval, output line, meter and click behaviour before choosing
    @{b}Add item@{ub}.
  * Or add another Command Tile and use Version as its command to make a
    simple version-status tile.
  * In novadock.cfg a tile is an ordinary plugin entry whose program is
    Plugins/Command, followed by its settings:

    PLUGIN=Free RAM|Plugins/Command|FREERAM|METER|ON
    OPTION.FREERAM.COMMAND=Avail TOTAL
    OPTION.FREERAM.INTERVAL=10

Rename or configure a tile with @{b}Edit...@{ub} in Organize or @{b}Edit settings...@{ub} from
the tile's context menu.

@{b}Settings@{ub}
--------

  @{b}Command@{ub}
      Meaning: Shell command line (max 95 characters)
      Default: Version
  @{b}Refresh seconds@{ub}
      Meaning: Time between runs, 1-86400
      Default: 60
  @{b}Output line@{ub}
      Meaning: Which non-empty output line to show, 1-20
      Default: 1
  @{b}Meter from output@{ub}
      Meaning: PERCENT uses the first number in that line (clamped to 0-100)
      as the tile's meter and graph
      Default: NONE
  @{b}Caption@{ub}
      Meaning: Small caption text; empty keeps the tile label
      Default: empty
  @{b}Click action@{ub}
      Meaning: RERUN runs the command now, COMMAND starts the click command,
      NONE
      Default: RERUN
  @{b}Click command@{ub}
      Meaning: Command for COMMAND clicks, e.g. an application to open
      Default: empty
  @{b}Timeout seconds@{ub}
      Meaning: When the tile reports TIMEOUT
      Default: 30

The shown text is cleaned up: console colour/escape codes and control
characters are removed, runs of spaces collapse, and the text is cut to the
tile's 31 characters. If a command prints nothing the tile shows NO OUTPUT,
or RC <n> when it failed.

Examples:

    Version                         Kickstart 47.115, Workbench 47.3
    Avail TOTAL       (line 1)      free memory in bytes
    Info DH0:  + Meter PERCENT      uses the first number on the line
    Date                            the current date and time
    C:Ping -c 1 192.168.1.1 (line 2) a round-trip line

@{b}How it runs commands@{ub}
--------------------

  * Each run starts an asynchronous shell with FailAt 2147483647 and Stack
    16384, runs the command with its output in T:NovaDockCmd-<ID>.out, then
    writes the return code to T:NovaDockCmd-<ID>.done. The plugin only polls
    for that file, so a slow command never blocks the plugin, the dock or
    other plugins, and the watchdog keeps seeing heartbeats.
  * A command that exceeds the timeout shows TIMEOUT. AmigaDOS has no safe
    way to kill another shell, so the next run starts only after it ends.
  * Clicks are delivered at once (the plugin sleeps with nd_client_wait).
  * Commands run with your own rights, exactly as typed in a Shell. Only
    configure commands you would run yourself; the tile never executes text
    from command output.
@endnode

@node SCRIPT_TILE "Script tiles"
@toc Main
@{b}Script tiles@{ub}
============

A Script tile shows whatever an ARexx script returns, so a status tile needs
no C compiler. Add one with @{b}Preferences > Add item... > Application > Type:
Script tile@{ub}. Each Script tile is its own Plugins/Script process with an ID
such as SCRIPT1; add as many as you like.

@{b}What the tile does@{ub}
------------------

Every @{i}Refresh seconds@{ui} the tile asks RexxMast to run the script as

    <script> UPDATE <id>

and when clicked (Click: @{i}Run script@{ui}) as <script> CLICK <id>. Use PARSE ARG
action id . to read them. The script's @{b}RESULT@{ub} becomes the tile:

  @{b}value@{ub}
      Tile: caption from the settings (or SCRIPT), the value
  @{b}caption@{ub}
      Tile: value
      : both texts
  @{b}caption@{ub}
      Tile: value
      : meter
      : plus a meter, 0-100 (empty or - for none)
  @{b}caption@{ub}
      Tile: value
      : meter
      : badge
      : plus a badge of up to 7 characters (empty removes it)

Texts are cut to 31 characters and control characters become spaces. A
script that ends without a RESULT keeps the tile's previous text, so a
script may also update the dock itself: its default host is NOVADOCK, and
commands such as 'SETBADGE' id '"3"' work directly.

NovaDock:Rexx/ScriptTile.rexx is a small example that shows the time, with
the part of the day that has passed as the meter, and the date when clicked.

@{b}Running and errors@{ub}
------------------

The script runs in RexxMast's own process. A slow script therefore never
stalls the tile or the dock, and the tile never starts a second run of the
same script while one is still going. The tile shows:

  @{b}NO AREXX@{ub}
      RexxMast is not running (start it from WBStartup or
      SYS:System/RexxMast)
  @{b}NO SCRIPT@{ub}
      No script set in the tile's settings
  @{b}TIMEOUT@{ub}
      No reply within @{i}Timeout seconds@{ui}; the result is still shown when it
      arrives
  @{b}RC n@{ub}
      The script failed with return code n (an ARexx error, or EXIT n)
  @{b}BAD RESULT@{ub}
      More than four fields, or a meter outside 0-100

@{b}Settings@{ub}
--------

  @{b}ARexx script@{ub}
      Meaning: Script path (REXX: and the current drawer are searched, .rexx
      may be omitted)
      Default: the example
  @{b}Refresh seconds@{ub}
      Meaning: 1-86400
      Default: 30
  @{b}Timeout seconds@{ub}
      Meaning: 1-600
      Default: 30
  @{b}Caption@{ub}
      Meaning: Caption when the RESULT has none
      Default: (empty: SCRIPT)
  @{b}Click action@{ub}
      Meaning: RUN calls the script with CLICK; NONE ignores clicks
      Default: RUN

Refresh intervals and dock heartbeats are independent. Long intervals
(including the default 30 seconds) do not trigger the 20-second stale-plugin
watchdog.
@endnode

@node DOPUS4_PLUGIN "Directory Opus 4"
@toc Main
@{b}Directory Opus 4@{ub}
================

NovaDock includes an optional DOpus4 plugin. It is installed with the other
plugins but is added through @{b}Add item > Plugins@{ub}, so existing docks do not
grow or start Directory Opus unexpectedly.

Add it from the dock background's @{b}Add item... -> Plugins@{ub} window. The tile
reports READY while DOpus's main user interface is visible, ICONIFIED while
DOpus is resident with its main screen closed, and OFFLINE when no instance
is present. The bridge checks the configured names and DOPUS.1 through
DOPUS.9, because DOpus selects the first free numbered port. Clicking an
iconified tile sends DOpus 4's native uniconify signal directly to the main
process that owns its ARexx port, waits for the screen to return, and then
sends DOPUSTOFRONT. This does not depend on the user's configured hotkey or
require changes to DOpus's defaults. Clicking a genuinely offline tile
starts the configured Directory Opus executable. While that launch is being
detected, the tile says STARTING; a failed launch or ARexx command is
reported briefly as START FAILED or FRONT FAILED.

This is a launcher and integration tile, not a meter. It therefore has no
Graph/Bar display mode and clicking it never changes the tile layout. Its
right-click menu matches an ordinary launcher with @{b}Launch@{ub} (start, restore,
or bring to front), @{b}Edit settings...@{ub}, and @{b}Remove@{ub}.

The settings are part of the normal novadock.cfg file:

    OPTION.DOPUS4.PORT=DOPUS.1
    OPTION.DOPUS4.ALT_PORT=DOPUS
    OPTION.DOPUS4.RESTORE_COMMAND=UNICONIFY
    OPTION.DOPUS4.FRONT_COMMAND=DOPUSTOFRONT
    OPTION.DOPUS4.PROGRAM=SYS:DirOpus4/DirectoryOpus
    OPTION.DOPUS4.CONFIG=
    OPTION.DOPUS4.POLL_TICKS=25

Use @{b}Edit...@{ub} on the plugin in Organize to adapt the port, commands or program
path to your installation. @{b}Select@{ub} beside Program path opens the native ASL
file requester, while the field remains editable for paths such as assigns.
The standard restore and front commands need no changes to the default DOpus
configuration. Leave @{b}Configuration file@{ub} empty to let Directory Opus use its
own normal default configuration. If a file is selected, NovaDock only
passes it to DOpus with -c; it never edits, replaces, or converts the file.

NovaDock deliberately avoids retaining palette pens for launcher artwork.
This allows the default DOpus custom screen to open after NovaDock on
palette-based and RTG systems without changing DOpus's screen settings.
NovaDock does not include Directory Opus itself and does not copy its code
or artwork.

The dock window uses simple refresh rather than a full-width smart-refresh
backing bitmap, and NovaDock releases its Workbench public-screen lock as
soon as the window has opened. This keeps display memory and screen
ownership available for DOpus's default Workbench-clone screen.

When CONFIG is empty, the plugin launches DirectoryOpus through Workbench,
not as a plain CLI command. This preserves the installed icon's standard
ICONSTART, BUTTONSTART, CONFIGFILE, and related tooltypes. Once DOpus's
ARexx port appears, the plugin brings its screen forward automatically. An
explicit CONFIG value still uses DOpus's documented -c command-line form.

The resident-task name, numbered-port search, uniconify signal, and
visibility handling mirror the GPL DOpus 4.16 implementation. They do not
require changing DOpus's default configuration.
@endnode

@node EYES_PLUGIN "Eyes"
@toc Main
@{b}Eyes@{ub}
====

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Installation " link EYES_PLUGIN 14}
  @{" Settings: right-click Eyes -> Plugin settings... " link EYES_PLUGIN 28}
  @{" Rendering and performance " link EYES_PLUGIN 44}
  @{" Limits and verification " link EYES_PLUGIN 61}

A lightweight, original Classic AmigaOS @{b}xeyes-inspired@{ub} tile: two eyes look
at the Workbench mouse pointer. No X11, external commodity, MUI, RTG or FPU.
It works with OS 3.0+ Intuition and graphics.library drawing on classic
palette-based ECS/AGA screens, subject to the unverified 68k build status.

@{b}Installation@{ub}
------------

The normal build.amiga builds Plugins/Eyes; install.amiga copies it to
NovaDock:Plugins/Eyes. It is included in the new sample novadock.cfg. For an
existing installation whose settings are preserved, append this line to the
@{b}single@{ub} config file:

    PLUGIN=Eyes|Plugins/Eyes|EYES|METER|ON

The bundled plugin reads and respects the standard plugin SETTINGS/QUIT
notifications. Eye painting and pointer sampling happen in the host rather
than sharing a RastPort with another process.

@{b}Settings: right-click Eyes -> Plugin settings...@{ub}
-----------------------------------------------

The plugin page in SYS:Prefs/NovaDockPrefs exposes these settings:

    OPTION.EYES.SIZE=NORMAL          # SMALL, NORMAL, LARGE
    OPTION.EYES.EYE_COLOR=WHITE      # WHITE, BLUE, GREEN
    OPTION.EYES.PUPIL_COLOR=DARK     # DARK, BLUE, GREEN
    OPTION.EYES.SENSITIVITY=100      # 10..100 (percentage of pupil travel)
    OPTION.EYES.SHOW_LABEL=NO        # YES or NO
    OPTION.EYES.BLINK=YES            # YES or NO
    OPTION.EYES.POLL_TICKS=20        # 20..250, 50 ticks = 1 second

Only novadock.cfg stores those values. ENV: holds the session copy (Use) and
ENVARC: holds the permanent copy (Save); no Eyes.cfg exists.

@{b}Rendering and performance@{ub}
-------------------------

  * The host uses timer.device at ~5 Hz when Eyes is present (or auto-hide
    is on), so tracking works if another Workbench window has focus.
  * POLL_TICKS limits Eye updates to 0.2..5 seconds; drawn pupil coordinates
    are compared before repaint to avoid unnecessary work.
  * Integer-only raster ellipses, theme palette pens and fixed 2-eye
    geometry keep it suitable for lower-colour native screens. Eye
    outline/center are intentionally simple rather than pixel-perfect X11
    anti-aliasing.
  * Blinking is optional and is an occasional one-frame animation. Eyes do
    not require an off-screen bitmap or a special accelerator.
  * If timer.device cannot be opened, the plugin tile can still show its
    static state, but mouse tracking will not refresh until another dock
    redraw occurs.

@{b}Limits and verification@{ub}
-----------------------

The plugin is a @{b}source prototype@{ub}. Pupil math, option validation and config
round-trips are host-tested, but vbcc native compilation and actual
Interactive Workbench testing remain outstanding. Classic AmigaOS uses no
modern process isolation; avoid untrusted third-party plugin binaries.

The widget is inspired by xeyes
(https://www.x.org/releases/X11R7.5/doc/man/man1/xeyes.1.html), not a port
of its X11 source code.
@endnode

@node MUSICPLAYER_PLUGIN "MusicPlayer"
@toc Main
@{b}MusicPlayer@{ub}
===========

MusicPlayer is one logical dock item for three separately installed AmigaOS
music players. On a top or bottom dock it spans two ordinary tile widths so
the module title and controls are not cramped. It remains one item for
selection, dragging, paging and configuration. Left and right docks retain a
normal single-column tile. MusicPlayer discovers known configurable ARexx
ports, allows manual choice, and can issue playback controls through
explicitly configured ARexx messages. Detected port presence @{b}does not@{ub}
guarantee known playback status.

While a supported player is detected, the dock tile itself shows compact
Previous, Play/Pause and Next buttons. A volume track is also shown when the
selected profile has both a volume query and an absolute volume command.
Click or drag along that track to set the level. Clicking elsewhere on the
tile is intentionally inert while a player is online, keeping the fixed
two-slot layout stable and avoiding a redundant second control window. When
no supported player is available, NovaDock shows the bundled musical-note
icon instead. Set DEFAULT_APP in the plugin settings and clicking that idle
tile starts the selected player.

Right-click the MusicPlayer tile -> @{b}Plugin settings...@{ub}. The unified
NovaDockPrefs provides paginated settings for MUSIC, including:

    OPTION.MUSIC.MODE=AUTO
    OPTION.MUSIC.PREFER=EAGLEPLAYER
    OPTION.MUSIC.DEFAULT_APP=SYS:Utilities/HippoPlayer/HippoPlayer
    OPTION.MUSIC.POLL_TICKS=100
    OPTION.MUSIC.AUTO_QUERY=YES
    OPTION.MUSIC.QUERY_TICKS=100
    OPTION.MUSIC.SCROLL_TITLE=YES
    OPTION.MUSIC.SCROLL_TICKS=25
    OPTION.MUSIC.TITLE_WIDTH=24
    OPTION.MUSIC.EAGLE_PORT=rexx_EP
    OPTION.MUSIC.EAGLE_QUERY_TITLE=STATUS M FIL
    OPTION.MUSIC.EAGLE_QUERY_VOLUME=STATUS G VOL
    OPTION.MUSIC.EAGLE_SET_VOLUME=VOLUME
    OPTION.MUSIC.EAGLE_VOLUME_MAX=255
    OPTION.MUSIC.AMIGAAMP_PORT=AMIGAAMP
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_PORT=HIPPOPLAYER
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_PLAY=CONT
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_PAUSE=STOP
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_PREVIOUS=CHOOSEPREV
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_NEXT=CHOOSENEXT
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_QUERY_TITLE=GET NAME
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_QUERY_PLAYING=GET PLAY
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_QUERY_VOLUME=GET VOLU
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_SET_VOLUME=VOLUME
    OPTION.MUSIC.HIPPO_VOLUME_MAX=64

All settings are in the @{b}same@{ub} novadock.cfg as the dock and the other plugins.
EaglePlayer 2.x is preconfigured with its documented rexx_EP ARexx port and
STATUS M FIL module-name query. Commands include optional EAGLE_QUERY_TITLE,
AMIGAAMP_QUERY_TITLE, volume up/down and other player-specific fields.
HippoPlayer is detected through its HIPPOPLAYER ARexx port. The bundled
profile follows the current HippoPlayer source interface: Previous/Next
first select the adjacent playlist entry and then issue PLAY; Play/Pause
uses CONT/STOP; GET NAME, GET PLAY and GET VOLU keep the tile title, button
state and 0..64 volume slider synchronized. These newer commands are
intended for HippoPlayer 2.47 or later. Select HIPPOPLAYER for MODE to force
it, or leave MODE at AUTO and set PREFER if several supported players run.

AmigaAMP commands remain version-dependent and should be checked against the
installed player. Automatic metadata queries are enabled for EaglePlayer.
EaglePlayer's bundled 2.06 guide documents STATUS G VOL and VOLUME 0..255,
which NovaDock converts to and from the tile's 0..100 percent slider.
HippoPlayer's documented 0..64 range is converted in the same way. AmigaAMP
volume fields remain empty by default because command syntax varies by
version; configure its query, set command and maximum before the slider is
enabled.

Inline actions travel back in the plugin's existing request/reply message.
They create no additional task, timer, public port or idle polling loop.

Old MusicPlayer.cfg files are not read in this release. Transfer customized
ports or command mappings into OPTION.MUSIC.* before upgrading. No player
binaries or copyrighted music are included.
@endnode

@node MIXER_PLUGIN "Mixer"
@toc Main
@{b}Mixer@{ub}
=====

The Mixer tile (Plugins/Mixer, ID MIXER) shows the AHI output volume and
changes it. It needs AHI 4 or later.

@{b}Using it@{ub}
--------

  * The tile shows the volume stored in AHI's preferences, 75% for example,
    with a matching meter, or MUTED. NO AHI means ENV:Sys/ahi.prefs was not
    found: open @{b}Prefs/AHI@{ub} and choose @{b}Save@{ub} once.
  * Click the tile to open a small window with a volume slider and a @{b}Mute@{ub}
    box. Release the slider to apply the new volume; @{b}Close@{ub}, Esc or Return
    closes the window. Click the tile again to close it as well.

@{b}How the volume is applied@{ub}
-------------------------

AHI has no system-wide "master volume" that every program shares. What it
does have is an output volume for the sound card, stored per unit in
ENV:Sys/ahi.prefs and sent to the card's driver whenever a program starts
using the default audio mode. The Mixer:

  1. writes the new volume to every unit in ENV:Sys/ahi.prefs;
  2. reopens ahi.device, which re-reads the file, and briefly allocates the
     default audio mode so the driver receives the new value at once;
  3. copies the file to ENVARC:Sys/ahi.prefs when the window closes (setting
     @{b}Remember volume after restart@{ub}), so a floppy or slow card is not written
     at every step.

The status line in the window says what happened:

  @{b}Applied@{ub}
      The driver has an output volume control and took the new value.
  @{b}Card in use: applies on next playback@{ub}
      Another program has the sound card (most drivers allow only one at a
      time). The value is saved and is used the next time a program starts
      playing.
  @{b}Driver has no volume control@{ub}
      This audio mode's driver reports no adjustable output volume (Paula,
      for example). The value is still stored for drivers and units that use
      it.
  @{b}ahi.device not available@{ub}
      AHI is not installed.

@{b}Settings@{ub}
--------

  @{b}Remember volume after restart@{ub}
      Meaning: Copy the changed preferences to ENVARC: when the window
      closes
      Default: YES
  @{b}Refresh ticks@{ub}
      Meaning: How often the tile re-reads the preferences (50 per second)
      Default: 100

Preference saves first write and close ahi.prefs.new, then replace the
target while preserving the previous complete file as ahi.prefs.bak. Failed
writes or closes leave the original intact. Failed replacement rolls back;
if that also fails, the complete original remains in .bak. Permanent-save
failures show SAVE FAILED and stay pending: reopen and close Mixer to retry.
If Mixer exits before saving succeeds, it displays an explanation after
releasing dock IPC. Check free space and write access to ENVARC:Sys.
@endnode

@node SCREENSHOT_PLUGIN "Screenshot"
@toc Main
@{b}Screenshot@{ub}
==========

The Screenshot tile (Plugins/Screenshot, ID SCREENSHOT) saves a screen as an
IFF ILBM picture that any Amiga paint program or viewer can load.

@{b}Using it@{ub}
--------

  1. Click the tile. It counts down (IN 2, IN 1), so you can bring another
     screen to the front first. A click during the countdown cancels it.
  2. The front screen (or Workbench, see Settings) is copied and saved as
     Screenshot-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.iff in the chosen drawer. A second shot
     within the same second gets -1, -2 and so on.
  3. The tile shows SAVED, and its badge counts the shots taken since the
     plugin started.

While a large screen is being written the tile shows SAVING n%.

@{b}What is saved@{ub}
-------------

  * The whole screen at its own resolution, with its palette (CMAP) and
    display mode (CAMG), ByteRun1 compressed, up to 256 colours.
  * The screen is copied with one blit while Intuition is locked, then
    written without the lock, so other programs are held up only for that
    copy. The copy needs as much memory as the screen itself; NO MEMORY
    means it was not available.
  * 15-, 16- and 24-bit RTG screens are not supported yet and report DEEP
    SCREEN. 8-bit RTG screens work.
  * The mouse pointer is a sprite and is not part of the picture.

@{b}Settings@{ub}
--------

  @{b}Save in drawer@{ub}
      Meaning: Where pictures are written; NO DRAWER if it does not exist
      Default: RAM:
  @{b}Countdown seconds@{ub}
      Meaning: 0-30; 0 saves immediately
      Default: 2
  @{b}Screen@{ub}
      Meaning: FRONT saves the frontmost screen, WORKBENCH the Workbench
      screen
      Default: FRONT
@endnode

@node SCREENMODE_PLUGIN "Screen Mode"
@toc Main
@{b}Screen Mode@{ub}
===========

The Screen Mode tile (Plugins/ScreenMode, ID SCREENMODE) shows the size of
the Workbench screen, for example 800x600x8, and switches between saved
ScreenMode presets.

@{b}Making presets@{ub}
--------------

Open @{b}Prefs/ScreenMode@{ub}, choose a mode, then @{b}Project > Save As...@{ub} and save it
in the presets drawer (by default SYS:Prefs/Presets) under a name such as
PAL Laced or RTG 1024. Repeat for each mode you want to switch between.

@{b}Switching@{ub}
---------

  1. Click the tile. A window lists every ScreenMode preset in the drawer
     with its size and display mode name.
  2. Select one and choose @{b}Use@{ub} (or press Return).
  3. The plugin runs ScreenMode FROM <preset> USE, the same as opening the
     preset in ScreenMode and choosing @{b}Use@{ub}, and Workbench reopens in the new
     mode. The setting is not saved to ENVARC:; open ScreenMode and choose
     @{b}Save@{ub} to keep it after a reboot.

Workbench can only change its screen while no other window is open on it.
The plugin therefore asks NovaDock to close its own windows first (the ARexx
command SUSPEND) and to reopen them (RESUME) once the new Workbench screen
is up, or after 12 seconds. Close other programs' windows on Workbench
first, or Intuition asks you to.

The tile shows SWITCHING meanwhile, and FAILED or NOT STARTED if ScreenMode
reported an error or could not be found.

@{b}Settings@{ub}
--------

  @{b}Presets drawer@{ub}
      Meaning: Drawer searched for ScreenMode preset files
      Default: SYS:Prefs/Presets
  @{b}ScreenMode program@{ub}
      Meaning: The ScreenMode preferences editor
      Default: SYS:Prefs/ScreenMode

The editor command runs in a separate worker while the tile keeps answering
the dock. A command that has not completed after 15 seconds shows COMMAND
TIMEOUT and the dock resumes. The worker is not forcibly killed: another
mode switch is refused until it exits (SWITCH STILL RUNNING). A late
completion is discarded after the waiting plugin has left; it cannot access
deleted reply ports. A failed SUSPEND aborts the operation before launching
the editor.
@endnode

@node TRASH_PLUGIN "Trash"
@toc Main
@{b}Trash@{ub}
=====

The Trash tile is the Workbench Trashcan on the dock. Add it with @{b}Add item >
Plugins > Trash@{ub} in NovaDock Preferences.

@{b}Using it@{ub}
--------

  @{b}Drop files or drawers on the tile@{ub}
      They move to the Trashcan of their own volume
  @{b}Click the tile@{ub}
      Opens the Trashcan on the system disk (SYS:Trashcan)
  @{b}Menu: Open trash@{ub}
      The same
  @{b}Menu: Empty trash...@{ub}
      Asks, then deletes everything in the trash
  @{b}Menu: Remove@{ub}
      Removes the tile from the dock

The tile shows EMPTY or the number of items, and a badge with the count.

@{b}How it works@{ub}
------------

Dropped objects are moved, never copied: each goes into the Trashcan drawer
of the volume it is on, together with its icon, so moving is instant and
cannot leave half-copied data. A volume without a Trashcan gets one, with
the standard Workbench trashcan icon. If an object of the same name is
already in the trash, the new one gets a .1, .2 suffix. Whole volumes and
objects already in a Trashcan are refused.

@{b}Empty trash...@{ub} names the number of items and asks before deleting anything;
deleting uses C:Delete ... ALL QUIET FORCE, so it also removes protected
files. It empties SYS:Trashcan and every Trashcan the plugin has used since
the last reboot. Other Trashcans can be emptied from Workbench.

The count is refreshed whenever the plugin moves or deletes something, not
on a timer, so the plugin never reads a floppy disk on its own. Objects put
in a Trashcan by Workbench itself are counted after the next drop or Empty.
With AmigaOS 3.5 or later, open Workbench windows update at once; on 3.0 and
3.1, use @{b}Window > Update@{ub} in Workbench.
@endnode

@node GAMES_PLUGIN "WHDLoad games"
@toc Main
@{b}WHDLoad games@{ub}
=============

The Games tile lists the WHDLoad games installed in a drawer and starts
them. Add it with @{b}Add item > Plugins > WHDLoad Games@{ub} in NovaDock
Preferences, and set @{b}Games drawer@{ub} in its settings (default Games:).

@{b}Using it@{ub}
--------

Click the tile to open the games list. Then:

  @{b}Type the first letters@{ub}
      Jump to the first game with that name
  @{b}Cursor up and down@{ub}
      Move through the list
  @{b}Return, Play, or a double-click@{ub}
      Start the game
  @{b}Rescan@{ub}
      Read the games drawer again, after installing games
  @{b}Esc or the close gadget@{ub}
      Close the list

The tile shows the number of games once the list has been read.

@{b}Which games it finds@{ub}
--------------------

A game is a drawer containing a WHDLoad .slave file, either directly in the
games drawer (Games:Turrican) or one level down, as in collections sorted by
letter (Games:T/Turrican). Its icon is <drawer>.info or <slave name>.info.
The drawer is read when the list is first opened, never when NovaDock
starts.

@{b}How games start@{ub}
---------------

The plugin starts a game exactly as a double-click on its icon would: it
loads the icon's default tool (normally WHDLoad, looked for beside the game
and in C:, SYS:C, SYS:Utilities, SYS:System and SYS:Tools) and runs it as a
Workbench program with the icon as its argument, so WHDLoad reads the icon's
tool types (PRELOAD, Quit key and so on). This works on AmigaOS 3.0 and
later. If NovaDock is quit while a game runs, the plugin waits for the game
to end before it exits.
@endnode

@node PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART "Plugin SDK quick start"
@toc Main
@{b}Plugin SDK quick start@{ub}
======================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Requirements " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 31}
  @{" Build the Hello example " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 41}
  @{" The complete simple plugin " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 63}
  @{" Turn the example into your plugin " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 93}
  @{" Settings window from the dock menu " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 112}
  @{" API levels " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 130}
  @{" Common mistakes " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 142}
  @{" Package and import a plugin " link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART 161}

The standalone SDK contains everything needed to build a basic NovaDock
status plugin without building NovaDock itself:

    NovaDock-PluginSDK/
      README.md
      Makefile
      include/nd_protocol.h
      include/nd_client.h
      src/nd_client.c
      include/nd_option_runtime.h
      src/nd_option_runtime.c
      examples/hello.c
      examples/Hello.ndplugin

Plugins are ordinary AmigaOS command-line programs. NovaDock starts a plugin
with its configured ID in argv[1]; the plugin sends small status messages to
the public NovaDock.1 Exec message port. NovaDock owns all tile drawing.

@{b}Requirements@{ub}
------------

  * Classic AmigaOS 3.0 or newer.
  * vbcc configured with an AmigaOS m68k target and compatible NDK headers.
  * A running NovaDock installation for testing.

The API targets the baseline 68000 and uses C89. No MUI, Reaction, shared
library, socket connection or NovaDock source build is required.

@{b}Build the Hello example@{ub}
-----------------------

Unpack the SDK, open its directory in an Amiga Shell, and run:

    make

If make is unavailable, enter this as one Shell line:

    vc -O -Iinclude -o Hello examples/hello.c src/nd_client.c src/nd_option_runtime.c

Copy the program and its manifest into NovaDock's plugin drawer:

    Copy Hello NovaDock:Plugins/Hello
    Copy examples/Hello.ndplugin NovaDock:Plugins/Hello.ndplugin

Open the dock's @{b}Add item > Plugins@{ub} window. Hello appears in the
installed-plugin list marked [New]; select it, choose @{b}Add item@{ub}, then @{b}Save@{ub}.
Its @{b}Settings@{ub} page already shows the Greeting and Refresh fields described by
the manifest. No hand-editing of novadock.cfg is needed. See
docs/PLUGIN_MANIFEST.md.

@{b}The complete simple plugin@{ub}
--------------------------

    #include <proto/dos.h>
    #include "nd_client.h"
    
    int main(int argc, char **argv)
    {
        struct NDClient client;
        unsigned int event;
        int clicked = 0;
    
        if (argc < 2 || !nd_client_init(&client, argv[1])) return 20;
        for (;;) {
            event = nd_client_update(&client, "HELLO",
                                     clicked ? "CLICKED!" : "HELLO AMIGA",
                                     ND_NO_METER);
            if (event & ND_EVENT_QUIT) break;
            if (event & ND_EVENT_CLICK) clicked = !clicked;
            Delay(nd_client_delay_ticks(&client,50));
        }
        nd_client_close(&client);
        return 0;
    }

The helper keeps one message in flight, waits for NovaDock's reply and
safely handles the public port disappearing. caption and value are limited
to 31 visible bytes plus the terminating NUL. A meter value is 0 through
100, or ND_NO_METER to hide it.

@{b}Turn the example into your plugin@{ub}
---------------------------------

  1. Copy examples/hello.c and rename the file and Makefile target.
  2. Keep nd_client.c, nd_client.h and nd_protocol.h unchanged.
  3. Replace the caption/value with your own short status text.
  4. Call nd_client_update() no more often than needed; once per second is a
     good default for status plugins.
  5. Handle ND_EVENT_CLICK if the tile should react to a normal click.
  6. Always handle ND_EVENT_QUIT and call nd_client_close() before exiting.
  7. Give every configured plugin instance a unique 1-19 character ID.

For progress meters, pass a percentage instead of ND_NO_METER. For one or
two normalized graph lines, use nd_client_update_graph(). Media integrations
can use nd_client_update_media() for Previous, Play/Pause, Next and volume.
If graph sampling must continue but identical values should not wake the
dock, use nd_client_update_graph_quiet() with a heartbeat shorter than 20
seconds.

@{b}Settings window from the dock menu@{ub}
----------------------------------

Right-click a plugin tile and choose @{b}Edit settings...@{ub} to open the shared
NovaDockPrefs editor for that plugin. You supply the fields in its manifest:

    NOVADOCK_OPTION=GREETING|Greeting|TEXT||HELLO AMIGA
    NOVADOCK_OPTION=POLL_TICKS|Refresh ticks|UINT|10,500|50

The SDK Hello example reads these values at startup and on
ND_EVENT_SETTINGS. Use applies them for the session; Save also saves the
permanent copy. You do not need to build a separate preferences executable.

Read @{"Creating your plugin's settings window" link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS} for all field types, a
configuration-reader example, import steps, testing and the current limits
on custom settings applications. This guide is also bundled with the
standalone SDK.

@{b}API levels@{ub}
----------

  * ABI v1: caption, value, meter, click, settings and quit events.
  * ABI v2: v1 plus two normalized graph samples.
  * ABI v3: v1 plus media capabilities, state, volume and control replies.

The v1 structure prefix remains unchanged. Choose the lowest API that
provides the feature you need. See docs/PLUGIN_API.md in the complete source
package for structure layout, lifecycle, validation, watchdog and
compatibility rules.

@{b}Common mistakes@{ub}
---------------

  * Do not cache the host port pointer; the supplied helper performs lookup
    and enqueue together under Forbid()/Permit().
  * Do not mutate or free an Exec message while it is in flight.
  * Do not send unterminated or oversized strings.
  * Do not busy-loop. Sleep between updates so the plugin remains
    inexpensive.
  * Do not perform dock drawing from a plugin. Send data and let NovaDock
    draw.
  * Do not ignore quit replies; disabled or removed plugins must exit
    promptly.

The SDK also contains nd_option_runtime.c/.h, so a plugin can read its own
OPTION.<ID>.<key> settings with nd_plugin_option_string() or
nd_plugin_options_effective(), and the example uses nd_client_wait() so it
reacts to clicks immediately.

@{b}Package and import a plugin@{ub}
---------------------------

Distribute the compiled executable with a manifest named after it,
<NOVADOCK_PROGRAM>.ndplugin, and optionally its icon:

    Hello-0.1/
      README.txt
      Plugins/
        Hello
        Hello.info          (optional)
        Hello.ndplugin

@{b}Import...@{ub} in Preferences > Plugins accepts a package only when its manifest
is complete: NOVADOCK_ID, NOVADOCK_VERSION, NOVADOCK_AUTHOR,
NOVADOCK_DESCRIPTION, NOVADOCK_LICENSE, NOVADOCK_FEATURES and
NOVADOCK_PROGRAM must all be present, the program name must match the
manifest's name, and the ID must not belong to a bundled plugin or to a
different installed plugin. Add NOVADOCK_MULTIPLE=YES only when separate
instances are safe. The README should state the plugin name, author,
license, compatible NovaDock version, ABI level and dependencies.

Import copies the program, its icon and the manifest into NovaDock's Plugins
drawer as one change: if anything fails, nothing is left half-installed and
a previous version stays in place. A newer version replaces an installed one
once its tiles are removed from the dock. @{b}Remove...@{ub} uninstalls a third-party
plugin that has no tiles, deleting its manifest first so a partly removed
plugin is never offered again. A plugin is a native executable, so install
packages only from a source you trust.
@endnode

@node PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS "Creating plugin settings windows"
@toc Main
@{b}Creating plugin settings windows@{ub}
================================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" 1. Declare the fields " link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS 15}
  @{" 2. Import and open the form " link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS 73}
  @{" 3. Read the values " link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS 97}
  @{" 4. Understand Use, Save and notification " link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS 136}
  @{" Custom preferences applications " link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS 153}

NovaDock already supplies the settings application. Declare your plugin's
fields in its manifest, and NovaDockPrefs builds the window opened by
@{b}right-clicking your dock tile > Edit settings...@{ub}. No separate preferences
executable or custom GadTools form is needed for ordinary options.

@{b}1. Declare the fields@{ub}
---------------------

Keep your executable and manifest together, with matching names. The Hello
example uses Hello and Hello.ndplugin. Retain the complete required metadata
from examples/Hello.ndplugin, then add option lines:

    NOVADOCK_OPTION=GREETING|Greeting|TEXT||HELLO AMIGA
    NOVADOCK_OPTION=POLL_TICKS|Refresh ticks|UINT|10,500|50

The format is:

    NOVADOCK_OPTION=KEY|Label|TYPE|Choices or range|Default

Use unique keys within the plugin. The manifest ID, such as HELLO,
identifies its configuration namespace. NovaDock passes the configured ID as
argv[1]; use that value rather than hard-coding it in your reader. A
manifest supports up to 12 option definitions.

  @{b}TEXT@{ub}
      Example: TITLE\\
      Settings control: Title\\
      : TEXT\\
      : \\
      : My tile
      : Editable text
  @{b}UINT@{ub}
      Example: POLL_TICKS\\
      Settings control: Refresh ticks\\
      : UINT\\
      : 10,500\\
      : 50
      : Validated integer within the range
  @{b}BOOL@{ub}
      Example: SHOW_LABEL\\
      Settings control: Show label\\
      : BOOL\\
      : \\
      : YES
      : Yes/No cycle
  @{b}CHOICE@{ub}
      Example: STYLE\\
      Settings control: Style\\
      : CHOICE\\
      : COMPACT,FULL\\
      : COMPACT
      : Cycle of the listed choices
  @{b}FILE@{ub}
      Example: PROGRAM\\
      Settings control: Program\\
      : FILE\\
      : \\
      : SYS:Utilities/MultiView
      : Editable path and a Select file button

The table omits the NOVADOCK_OPTION= prefix for readability. Defaults must
match their declared types. Do not put a pipe character inside a field.

@{b}2. Import and open the form@{ub}
---------------------------

  1. Build the SDK Hello example with make.
  2. In NovaDock Preferences, open @{b}Plugins > Import...@{ub} and select its
     manifest.
  3. Review the import preview and choose @{b}Add to dock...@{ub}.
  4. Right-click the new tile and choose @{b}Edit settings...@{ub}.
  5. Change Greeting or Refresh ticks, then choose @{b}Use@{ub} or @{b}Save@{ub}.

The dock launches the shared editor with arguments of this form:

    NovaDockPrefs PAGE=PLUGIN PLUGIN=HELLO FOCUSED=1

PLUGIN= must be the actual configured ID. The focused editor shows that
plugin's declared fields; a plugin with no fields shows an explanatory
message. The window sizes itself for the Workbench screen and scrolls when
necessary.

After changing a manifest's schema, remove its dock tiles and re-import the
updated package so Preferences receives the new definitions, then add the
tile again. Editing only the manifest on disk does not update an already
imported configuration's schema.

@{b}3. Read the values@{ub}
------------------

The SDK's examples/hello.c already implements the complete flow. Include
nd_option_runtime.h and batch related values into one configuration read:

    static void read_settings(const char *id, char *greeting,
                              unsigned int size, unsigned int *poll)
    {
        struct NDOptionString fields[2];
        char ticks[8];
    
        fields[0].key = "GREETING";
        fields[0].fallback = "HELLO AMIGA";
        fields[0].out = greeting;
        fields[0].limit = size;
        fields[1].key = "POLL_TICKS";
        fields[1].fallback = "50";
        fields[1].out = ticks;
        fields[1].limit = sizeof(ticks);
        nd_plugin_option_strings(id, fields, 2);
        *poll = (unsigned int)atoi(ticks);
        if (*poll < 10 || *poll > 500) *poll = 50;
    }

This excerpt needs <stdlib.h> and nd_option_runtime.h. Call it once after
initializing the client, and again after an update reports settings changes:

    if (event & ND_EVENT_QUIT) break;
    if (event & ND_EVENT_SETTINGS)
        read_settings(argv[1], greeting, sizeof(greeting), &poll);

Use nd_client_wait(&client, nd_client_delay_ticks(&client, poll)) in the
update loop so the dock can wake the plugin for changes. Link
src/nd_client.c and src/nd_option_runtime.c, as the SDK Makefile does. The
helper handles configuration-source selection; do not assume that the active
file always lives beside your executable. Validate values in your plugin
too, since users can edit configuration files manually.

@{b}4. Understand Use, Save and notification@{ub}
----------------------------------------

  * @{b}Use@{ub} applies settings for the current session.
  * @{b}Save@{ub} applies them and saves the permanent copy.
  * Closing the editor discards changes that have not been applied.
  * The shared editor writes OPTION.<ID>.<KEY> records to NovaDock's single
    configuration. Plugins should read those records, not rewrite the whole
    file.
  * ND_EVENT_SETTINGS means applied settings changed. It is not a request
    for your plugin to open a preferences window. The dock may also restart
    a plugin for some changes, so always read settings at startup as well.

Check that changing Greeting updates the tile without restarting the dock,
that an invalid refresh value is rejected or safely replaced by a default,
and that Save survives quitting and restarting NovaDock.

@{b}Custom preferences applications@{ub}
-------------------------------

The current manifest has no hook to replace @{b}Edit settings...@{ub} with an
external preferences executable. Do not invent a NOVADOCK_PREFS field or use
ND_EVENT_SETTINGS as an open-window event.

If your plugin needs a specialised UI, it can own a native
Intuition/GadTools window or launch its own helper through a plugin action,
such as handling ND_EVENT_CLICK. Keep processing dock updates and honour
ND_EVENT_QUIT while that UI is open. Avoid a long blocking window loop in
the update task; use a separate worker or integrate the window's signals
with the plugin event loop. Such a UI does not automatically replace the
dock's right-click settings form.
@endnode

@node PLUGIN_API "Plugin protocol"
@toc Main
@{b}Plugin protocol@{ub}
===============

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Running a plugin " link PLUGIN_API 34}
  @{" ABI layout " link PLUGIN_API 55}
  @{" Fast path: helper client " link PLUGIN_API 117}
  @{" Reducing idle traffic for stable status tiles " link PLUGIN_API 145}
  @{" Adding graph data from a third-party plugin " link PLUGIN_API 185}
  @{" Safety and stability " link PLUGIN_API 228}
  @{" Bundled examples " link PLUGIN_API 257}
  @{" NovaDock 0.1 preferences and control port " link PLUGIN_API 307}
  @{" Network connection example " link PLUGIN_API 361}
  @{" MusicPlayer ARexx bridge example (0.1) " link PLUGIN_API 369}
  @{" One-file settings API (0.1) " link PLUGIN_API 382}
  @{" Bundled xeyes-style Eyes tile " link PLUGIN_API 393}
  @{" Badges (ABI 4, optional) " link PLUGIN_API 407}
  @{" Waking on clicks (optional) " link PLUGIN_API 437}
  @{" Second event word (ABI 5, optional) " link PLUGIN_API 462}
  @{" Settings and long configuration lines " link PLUGIN_API 482}

NovaDock plugins are @{b}ordinary, independent AmigaOS 68k executable processes@{ub}.
There are no undocumented jumps into dock memory, no shared C runtime, and
no need to ship a custom .library. A plugin communicates using exec.library
message ports; the dock draws the tiles. A faulty plugin is much less likely
to corrupt dock internals than a directly linked binary plugin, though
native AmigaOS has no modern process memory protection.

For a standalone build kit and a first-plugin walkthrough, start with
@{"PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART.md" link PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART}. The public download named
NovaDock-0.1-plugin-sdk.zip contains only the public headers, safe client
helper, Makefile and Hello example; building the complete dock is not
required.

@{b}Running a plugin@{ub}
----------------

Add a line to novadock.cfg, then reload via NovaDockPrefs (Use/Save) or
restart NovaDock:

    PLUGIN=My Widget|Plugins/MyWidget|WIDGET1

The fields are the initial tile label, executable path, and unique plugin
ID. Relative paths are resolved from NovaDock's executable directory by the
host. Absolute paths such as Work:Widgets/CPU are permitted. The host starts
the program asynchronously and passes the plugin ID as @{b}argv[1]@{ub}. IDs have
1-19 ASCII letters, digits, -, _; no spaces. Plugins must be CLI
executables; the dock launches them, not Workbench.

@{b}The configuration explicitly enables each plugin.@{ub} Preferences discovers
self-describing plugins from the installed Plugins drawer. Plugin IDs are
unique by default. A manifest may opt into repeated dock tiles with
NOVADOCK_MULTIPLE=YES; those views intentionally share one process, one live
state and one settings namespace rather than starting competing clients.

@{b}ABI layout@{ub}
----------

See include/nd_protocol.h for the complete NDMessage wire structure. The
original message prefix remains protocol ND_ABI_VERSION = 1. The optional
graph protocol accepts ND_GRAPH_ABI_VERSION = 2 with two appended samples.
The optional media protocol accepts ND_MEDIA_ABI_VERSION = 3 with control
capabilities, play state, volume and a reply-side requested volume. @{b}v1
binaries remain valid@{ub}; graph and media functionality are opt-in and v1
progress meters can be displayed as a single-line graph.

  * The host registers the named public port NovaDock.1.
  * A plugin allocates a private reply MsgPort, fills one NDMessage, and
    sends it to the host with PutMsg.
  * FindPort and PutMsg occur together under Forbid/Permit: never cache a
    public port pointer outside this protected critical section.
  * mn_ReplyPort must point to the sender's live reply port; mn_Length =
    sizeof(struct NDMessage).
  * On ND_MSG_UPDATE, the host copies caption, value, and meter into its own
    tile model. Each char array must be NUL terminated.
  * meter ranges 0-100 (shown as a progress strip); 65535 hides it.
  * ND_EVENT_THROTTLE is an additive v1-compatible reply hint indicating
    that auto-hide currently shows only the reveal tab. Headless plugins may
    call nd_client_delay_ticks() with their normal delay to obtain a
    watchdog-safe 2..10-second backoff. Older plugins can ignore the bit.
    Interactive plugin windows should retain their normal event cadence.
  * For v1 messages, the same meter can also be plotted as a one-line
    history graph when the tile is configured GRAPH or toggled with Shift +
    click.
  * For v2 messages, the appended graph_a and graph_b fields provide
    @{b}independent@{ub} 0-100 chart samples; 65535 creates a gap (no data). The dock
    must check the message version @{b}and@{ub} mn_Length before accessing these
    fields. Incomplete v2 messages are rejected.
  * For v3 messages, capabilities, state and volume let the host draw inline
    Previous, Play/Pause, Next and volume controls. Their actions are
    returned through ND_EVENT_MEDIA_*; an absolute slider request is
    returned in requested_volume. Incomplete v3 messages are rejected.
  * The dock ReplyMsgs the same message. The event field in that reply uses
    ND_EVENT_NONE, ND_EVENT_CLICK, ND_EVENT_QUIT, optional settings, and the
    v3 media-action bits.
  * The @{b}next update@{ub} after a user clicks a plugin tile receives CLICK, except
    for host-owned interactive surfaces such as MusicPlayer's inline
    controls. The plugin may use this to toggle modes (as the clock/RAM
    plugins do).
  * On QUIT, or when the named host port disappears, the plugin should exit.
  * A plugin can send ND_MSG_GOODBYE before exiting. The dock then shows it
    as offline; on a normal dock exit the public port is removed and all
    queued plugin messages are replied to with QUIT.

Messages are always sent @{b}one at a time@{ub} by the bundled client helper. Never
free or mutate a sent message until its reply arrives. If implementing your
own client, ensure the message has the memory lifetime necessary for
asynchronous Exec message passing. Plugins should sleep between updates, for
example with Delay(50) to refresh once per second.

The ABI is currently @{b}single instance@{ub}: only one dock publishes the name
NovaDock.1. It deliberately uses native 68k integer sizes and structure
alignment, and is not a network or cross-endian protocol. Third-party
programs can use another compiler, but must match the NDK struct layout and
version. The caption field is display text chosen by the plugin, distinct
from the configured initial label.

@{b}Fast path: helper client@{ub}
------------------------

See the complete template in plugin_src/template/hello.c. The standalone SDK
places the same canonical source at examples/hello.c:

    struct NDClient client;
    unsigned int event;
    nd_client_init(&client, argv[1]);
    for (;;) {
        event = nd_client_update(&client, "HELLO", "42", ND_NO_METER);
        if (event & ND_EVENT_QUIT) break;
        Delay(50);
    }
    nd_client_close(&client);

Link plugin_src/common/nd_client.c into your own executable and include the
headers from include/ and plugin_src/common/.

    vc -O -Iinclude -Iplugin_src/common -o Plugins/Hello \\
      plugin_src/template/hello.c plugin_src/common/nd_client.c

Amiga Shell commands need to be entered as one line rather than using the
Unix backslash continuation above. The standalone SDK also includes a
ready-to-use Makefile, so make builds Hello directly.

Then add PLUGIN=Hello|Plugins/Hello|HELLO1 to the dock configuration.

@{b}Reducing idle traffic for stable status tiles@{ub}
---------------------------------------------

Status-only plugins do not need to exchange an identical packet on every
poll. The client helper provides nd_client_update_quiet, which sends
immediately when the caption, value or meter changes and otherwise sends
only the requested heartbeat:

    /* The loop sleeps 10 ticks; 100 ticks gives an unchanged tile a 2 s heartbeat. */
    event = nd_client_update_quiet(&client, "STATUS", value, ND_NO_METER,
                                   10, 100);
    if (event & ND_EVENT_QUIT) break;
    Delay(10);

elapsed_ticks is the time slept since the preceding call, not the absolute
system time. A skipped exchange returns ND_EVENT_NONE. Keep the heartbeat
comfortably below the host's 20-second stale timeout; 100 ticks (two
seconds) is the recommended stable-tile default.

Graph plugins can sample at their normal cadence without sending unchanged
samples by using nd_client_update_graph_quiet. It has the same graph values
as nd_client_update_graph, followed by elapsed_ticks and heartbeat_ticks.
The bundled CPU and RAM plugins sample once per second but send only changed
values or a ten-second heartbeat.

Use the normal update helpers for plugins that must receive every click
immediately. Plugin actions are returned in the reply to the next exchange,
so a quiet heartbeat is also the maximum input latency while an unchanged
tile is idle. The bundled Clock, Eyes and Archive Extractor use a two-second
idle heartbeat. DOpus 4 uses one to two seconds so its start/front action
remains quick. MusicPlayer keeps its control-driven exchanges.

The helper remembers the latest host pacing hint:

    Delay(nd_client_delay_ticks(&client, normal_poll_ticks));

The returned delay equals the configured value while visible. While hidden
it is four times longer, clamped to 100..500 AmigaDOS ticks (2..10 seconds),
which remains comfortably inside the 20-second watchdog interval.

@{b}Adding graph data from a third-party plugin@{ub}
-------------------------------------------

In novadock.cfg, enable the sparkline on just the intended tiles:

    PLUGIN=Example|Plugins/Example|EXAMPLE|GRAPH
    PLUGIN=Clock|Plugins/Clock|CLOCK|METER

Hold @{b}Shift@{ub} and left-click a plugin tile at runtime to switch between graph
and meter without affecting a plugin's own click handler. View toggles are
not currently persisted; the optional fourth field sets the startup mode.

For @{b}one graph series@{ub}, the v1 helper is sufficient: its meter can be sampled
as history even if the current display is set to METER. To supply @{b}two
distinct traces@{ub}, use the v2 helper:

    /* Both series and the existing bar are independently normalized to 0..100. */
    event = nd_client_update_graph(&client, "NET", "14/7 p/s",
                                   14, 14, 7);
    /* Use ND_GRAPH_NO_SAMPLE for absent/invalid data or a single series. */

For a graph that is often unchanged, use the quiet form:

    event = nd_client_update_graph_quiet(&client, "NET", "14/7 p/s",
                                         14, 14, 7,
                                         elapsed_ticks, 500);

Here 500 ticks provides a ten-second heartbeat while changed samples are
sent immediately.

The graph API is defined in include/nd_protocol.h, and the implementation is
in plugin_src/common/nd_client.c. struct NDGraphMessage begins with an
unchanged struct NDMessage prefix; graph_a and graph_b are appended UWORD
values. Sending a graph-ABI message to a host that only implements ABI v1 is
not supported; use nd_client_update (v1) for widest backward compatibility.

The host stores @{b}96 samples per tile@{ub} in a fixed-size ring buffer and draws
pixels/lines itself using graphics.library. Each update advances the graph
by one sample. These are normalized samples, @{b}not arbitrary bitmap draws@{ub} or
raw bytes-per-second readings. At 1 update/sec a full graph spans about 96
seconds. When a plugin goes offline its history is cleared. A regular click
remains a plugin event, while Shift + click is consumed by the dock.

@{b}Safety and stability@{ub}
--------------------

  * Plugins are not sandboxed: treat third-party executables like any other
    Amiga program, and get them from trusted authors.
  * Plugin IDs are a routing key, @{b}not authentication@{ub}. Another local task
    with access to the public port can spoof messages.
  * The host owns all drawing and doesn't use plugin-supplied bitmap
    pointers.
  * IPC updates include a fixed-size, bounded caption and value; no
    arbitrary GUI calls are performed by the host on the plugin's behalf.
  * The host validates message size, magic, protocol version, and a
    configured ID.
  * Messages are synchronous from the helper client, with a fifteen-second
    timer.device deadline (ND_PLUGIN_IPC_TIMEOUT_SECONDS). If the public
    dock port exists but stops replying, the helper returns ND_EVENT_QUIT so
    the plugin can exit. The helper allocates one public wire packet at
    startup and reuses it for every normal exchange.
  * After a timeout, the dock may still own the outstanding message. The
    helper therefore turns the private reply port into a signal-free
    PA_IGNORE target and deliberately leaves that one packet and port valid
    until reboot instead of risking a use-after-free. This exceptional leak
    is bounded to one timed-out exchange per plugin process; the normal and
    missing-host paths release all resources.
  * ABI v1 supports short text, progress bars, and click callbacks. The
    optional ABI v2 extension adds two numeric graph samples, but @{b}not
    arbitrary canvas drawing@{ub}. Custom canvases, notifications, plugin
    permissions and hot reload remain future work.

@{b}Bundled examples@{ub}
----------------

  @{b}Clock@{ub}
      Display: Current system time
      Click behavior: Toggle 24h/12h
      Notes: Reads AmigaDOS DateStamp
  @{b}Activity@{ub}
      Display: CPU used percentage with graph
      Click behavior: Shift-click graphs
      Notes: EClock time outside a lowest-priority Exec task
  @{b}Memory@{ub}
      Display: Separate Chip (G) and Fast (F) used-total meters and graphs
      Click behavior: Per-tile settings
      Notes: Uses AvailMem; Chip is Workbench's graphics mem; legacy RAM IDs
      still support all/chip selection
  @{b}Network@{ub}
      Display: RX/TX packets/sec, dual graph
      Click behavior: Cycle RX/TX, RX and TX
      Notes: Reads a configured SANA-II interface; OPTION.NET.* in the
      unified novadock.cfg
  @{b}ArchiveExtractor@{ub}
      Display: Installed unpackers and latest result
      Click behavior: Drop an archive
      Notes: Host-routed ZIP, LHA/LZH, RAR and ARJ extraction with a live
      Shell progress window; see ARCHIVE_PLUGIN.md
  @{b}Hello@{ub}
      Display: Text from example plugin
      Click behavior: Toggle text
      Notes: SDK template, not enabled by default

Archive drops are a bundled host integration rather than a new wire-protocol
feature: NovaDock validates the Workbench drop, then starts the same
ArchiveExtractor executable in short-lived helper mode. Third-party plugin
ABI compatibility is unchanged.

@{b}Network meter terminology:@{ub} S2_GETGLOBALSTATS supplies aggregate packet
counters, not global byte totals. The network plugin reads a configured
SANA-II device without an exclusive open and calculates RX/TX packets/sec.
SCALE_PPS controls the bar scale, not bandwidth usage. See the README for
configuration and driver limitations. Its parser/rate implementation lives
in plugin_src/network/nd_network.c and include/nd_network.h.

@{b}CPU meter terminology:@{ub} Activity briefly wakes a lowest-priority Exec task
and uses its task-switch callbacks to accumulate EClock time spent idle
during a 0.1-second sample. Each sample displays 100 - idle_percent, clamped
to 0-100. The task blocks for the rest of the update interval instead of
continuously sampling idle time. A task monitor may attribute the free part
of that short window to NovaDock CPU sample (free); it is not NovaDock load.

@{b}NovaDock 0.1 preferences and control port@{ub}
-----------------------------------------

The independent preferences executable does @{b}not@{ub} send plugin messages to
NovaDock.1. It writes text to ENV:NovaDock/novadock.cfg for @{b}Use@{ub} or both ENV:
and ENVARC: for @{b}Save@{ub}, then sends a synchronous versioned control message to
the separate NovaDock.Control.1 port. See include/nd_control.h and
src/control_client.c. The host replies with a status code; a missing dock is
not an error to the preferences editor. Ordinary commands and Diagnostics
requests use a five-second timer.device deadline. Reload uses its own
bounded 20-second deadline because it completes the window, artwork, colour,
font and plugin rebuild before acknowledging Preferences. ND_CONTROL_TIMEOUT
is distinct from an invalid configuration, and the client's heap-backed
packet/reply target remains valid if a wedged dock still owns it after
either deadline.

ND_CONTROL_DIAGNOSTICS_RESET starts a new measurement baseline. It clears
telemetry totals and per-plugin diagnostic counts, but deliberately retains
plugin liveness timestamps, graph history, online state, watchdog
retry/backoff state and configuration. The Preferences Diagnostics page can
also save its current human-readable rows to RAM:NovaDock-Diagnostics.txt;
this export is local to Preferences and does not add another control-port
message format.

The optional fifth PLUGIN config field is ON (default) or OFF:

    PLUGIN=Example|Plugins/Example|EXAMPLE|GRAPH|ON
    PLUGIN=Disabled|Plugins/Disabled|DISABLED|METER|OFF

IDs remain unique across enabled and disabled entries. The editor can
reorder entries, flip ON/OFF and GRAPH/METER without altering the executable
path or plugin IDs; paths and IDs may still be edited manually. Existing
v1/v2 plugin binaries do not need to link any preferences client code.

A removed/disabled plugin receives ND_EVENT_QUIT at its next ordinary status
update. Existing IDs retain their plugin instance when reloading; changing
the executable path for an existing ID requires restarting the dock. The
current ABI lacks authenticated registration and instance generation
identifiers, so immediate OFF->ON or recovery of a genuinely hung task can
briefly duplicate a process. Plugins should poll and honor ND_EVENT_QUIT
promptly rather than relying on host shutdown or direct task killing.

After a blocking reload, online plugins receive a fresh 20-second watchdog
window; diagnostics still show their actual last update time.

The host checks heartbeats on its existing low-rate housekeeping timer; it
does not add a second polling task. An enabled plugin that produces no
update for 20 seconds is marked stale and relaunched. ND_MSG_GOODBYE
schedules a quicker five-second retry. Repeated failures wait 20, 40, 80,
160 and then 300 seconds between attempts, preventing a bad path or broken
executable from creating a shell-launch storm. Plugins with unusually slow
work should still send a lightweight status heartbeat inside the 20-second
window.

@{b}Network connection example@{ub}
--------------------------

plugin_src/network/ combines traffic sampling with Roadshow connection
controls. Its shared nd_roadshow.c backend validates command arguments; its
independent worker owns connection and Details windows. See
@{"NETWORK_PLUGIN.md" link NETWORK_PLUGIN}. No standalone Roadshow plugin is shipped.

@{b}MusicPlayer ARexx bridge example (0.1)@{ub}
--------------------------------------

plugin_src/musicplayer/ uses the optional v3 media extension while retaining
the unchanged v1 message prefix. It detects EaglePlayer, AmigaAMP or
HippoPlayer ARexx public message ports. Its fixed-width tile is the control
surface: transport buttons and a capability-gated volume slider are
available directly in the dock, while clicks on non-control space are
intentionally inert. Player selection and ARexx actions stay inside the
separate plugin process. The nd_musicplayer.c config/selection component
builds with portable C89. See @{"MUSICPLAYER_PLUGIN.md" link MUSICPLAYER_PLUGIN} for setup, selection,
ARexx limitations, and upgrade instructions.

@{b}One-file settings API (0.1)@{ub}
---------------------------

All options are stored in novadock.cfg. Right-click a plugin -> @{b}Plugin
settings...@{ub} to open the plugin page in NovaDockPrefs. Custom modules may
declare
OPTIONDEF=ID|KEY|Label|BOOL/CHOICE/UINT/TEXT|choices-or-range|default inside
that file and receive ND_EVENT_SETTINGS on changed preferences. Existing ABI
1/2 message structs have not changed; v3 is additive. See
docs/PLUGIN_SETTINGS.md and include/nd_option_runtime.h.

@{b}Bundled xeyes-style Eyes tile@{ub}
-----------------------------

plugin_src/eyes/eyes.c is a regular v1-ABI plugin process. The host has a
special-case native widget for plugin ID EYES, using src/eyes.c for bounded
integer pupil calculation and Intuition/graphics raster drawing. This is not
a general plugin-provided custom drawing API: third-party plugins continue
to submit text, meter or graph samples to the host. The separate Eyes
executable is still needed for the dock to report it online and show the
tracking display.

Seven settings are declared in src/nd_options.c and kept in the same
novadock.cfg; native Preferences provides editing automatically.

@{b}Badges (ABI 4, optional)@{ub}
------------------------

A plugin can show a small label on its tile's top-right corner, for example
an unread count:

    event |= nd_client_set_badge(&client, "3");   /* show "3" */
    event |= nd_client_set_badge(&client, "*");   /* a plain dot */
    event |= nd_client_set_badge(&client, "");    /* remove it */

Badges are up to seven characters, drawn in the theme's secondary accent
colour, and shown as a dot on tiny tiles or when the text does not fit. They
take the place of the running dot in the same corner, appear on every tile
of a multi-tile plugin, and disappear when the plugin says goodbye. Call
nd_client_set_badge() only when the badge changes; it sends nothing when the
text is unchanged.

Older docks answer unknown commands with QUIT, so badges use a handshake: a
dock with badge support sets ND_EVENT_HOST_BADGES on every reply, and the
helper sends ND_MSG_BADGE only after seeing it. A badge set earlier is held
and sent automatically. On an older dock the badge is simply never shown and
the plugin keeps working, so using badges does not require NOVADOCK_ABI=4.

Without the helper, send a v1 header with command=ND_MSG_BADGE and the badge
text in value (caption and meter are ignored), and only to a dock whose
replies carry ND_EVENT_HOST_BADGES.

Always test reply events with &: new docks may set bits your plugin does not
know about, such as ND_EVENT_HOST_BADGES.

@{b}Waking on clicks (optional)@{ub}
---------------------------

Plugins traditionally sleep with Delay(), so a click waited up to one poll
interval. The client library now provides

    unsigned int nd_client_wait(struct NDClient *c, unsigned int ticks);

It sleeps for up to ticks (1/50 s) but returns as soon as NovaDock signals
ND_WAKE_SIGNAL (SIGBREAKF_CTRL_F), which the dock sends to the task owning
the plugin's reply port when a click, media action or settings change is
queued. The next exchange then delivers the event. The return value is the
number of ticks that actually elapsed and can be passed straight to
nd_client_update_quiet(). The message ABI is unchanged; plugins that keep
using Delay() simply ignore the signal. Do not use SIGBREAKF_CTRL_F for
anything else in a plugin's main task.

A plugin built with the current client library also publishes a signal-less
public port named NovaDock.Wake.<ID> (ND_WAKE_PORT_PREFIX) whose mp_SigTask
is the plugin's process. NovaDock then signals the plugin only while that
port still names the task from the latest exchange, so an exited plugin's
task pointer is never signalled, even if Exec has reused its memory.
nd_client_init() and nd_client_close() manage the port; plugins built with
an older client keep the earlier task-pointer wake-up.

@{b}Second event word (ABI 5, optional)@{ub}
-----------------------------------

Every bit of the 16-bit event field is in use. ABI 5 adds a superset packet,
struct NDExtMessage, whose reply carries a 32-bit event2. A dock that
understands it sets the priority (ln_Pri) of its public NovaDock port to
ND_HOST_ABI (5); older docks leave it at 0. nd_client reads that value when
it finds the port and only then sends v5 packets, so a plugin built with the
current client works unchanged with older docks. Existing calls
(nd_client_update, _graph, _media and their quiet variants) need no change.
Collect the new events with

    unsigned long nd_client_events2(struct NDClient *c); /* ND_EVENT2_*, clears */

which is always 0 with an older dock. ND_EVENT2_REFRESH asks for a fresh
value now. Writing the packet by hand: set version to ND_EXT_ABI_VERSION,
mn_Length to sizeof(struct NDExtMessage), and flags to ND_EXT_HAS_GRAPH
and/or ND_EXT_HAS_MEDIA for the fields you filled in, and send it only when
the dock port's ln_Pri is at least 5.

@{b}Settings and long configuration lines@{ub}
-------------------------------------

nd_plugin_options_effective() first reads ENV:NovaDock/novadock.source,
which the running dock writes with the path of the file it actually loaded
(possibly a recovered .bak generation). Lines longer than the reader's
buffer are skipped rather than aborting the read.

@{b}Timeout ownership and graph timing@{ub}

Current clients allocate explicitly owned reply ports. On timeout they
return only the signal bit and leave an inert port while the dock owns the
packet. The updated dock reclaims that port and packet when it finishes the
request, including during shutdown. A reply that won the timeout race is
reclaimed by the sender instead. This keeps delayed replies safe even after
the sender exits. Older hosts cannot perform this reclamation; ownership
must remain retained if a legacy or crashed receiver never returns it.

Graph history is indexed by received samples. Adaptive polling and quiet
updates vary the wall-clock distance between columns; it is not a
fixed-seconds time axis. Diagnostics labels this explicitly.
@endnode

@node PLUGIN_MANIFEST "Plugin manifests"
@toc Main
@{b}Plugin manifests@{ub}
================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Writing a manifest " link PLUGIN_MANIFEST 14}
  @{" What Preferences does with it " link PLUGIN_MANIFEST 86}
  @{" Reading the settings in the plugin " link PLUGIN_MANIFEST 106}
  @{" Plugin Manager " link PLUGIN_MANIFEST 121}

A NovaDock plugin can describe itself, so users install it by copying files
instead of editing novadock.cfg. When NovaDockPrefs starts, it reads the
descriptions in the Plugins drawer and offers every new plugin in @{b}Add
item... > Plugins@{ub}, together with a ready-made settings page.

@{b}Writing a manifest@{ub}
------------------

Put NOVADOCK_ lines either in a text file named after the program with the
suffix .ndplugin, or as tool types in the program's own icon:

    Plugins/Weather            the plugin executable
    Plugins/Weather.ndplugin   text manifest  (or)
    Plugins/Weather.info       icon with the same lines as tool types

    NOVADOCK_ID=WEATHER
    NOVADOCK_NAME=Weather
    NOVADOCK_VIEW=GRAPH
    NOVADOCK_ABI=2
    NOVADOCK_MULTIPLE=YES
    NOVADOCK_OPTION=CITY|City|TEXT||Stockholm
    NOVADOCK_OPTION=UNITS|Units|CHOICE|C,F|C
    NOVADOCK_OPTION=POLL_TICKS|Refresh ticks|UINT|250,30000|3000

  @{b}NOVADOCK_ID@{ub}
      Meaning: Required. Unique ID (letters, digits, _, -, max 19). The dock
      passes it in argv[1], and settings are stored as OPTION.<ID>.<key>.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_NAME@{ub}
      Meaning: Tile label; defaults to the program's file name.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_VIEW@{ub}
      Meaning: METER (default) or GRAPH, the initial view.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_ABI@{ub}
      Meaning: Lowest dock IPC ABI the plugin needs: 1 (basic), 2 (graphs),
      3 (media controls) or 4 (badges). A newer requirement is reported, not
      installed. Badges degrade gracefully, so a plugin that only adds a
      badge can keep a lower ABI.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_MULTIPLE@{ub}
      Meaning: YES allows several dock tiles for this plugin; the default is
      NO. Repeated tiles share one plugin process, live state and settings
      namespace.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_VERSION@{ub}
      Meaning: Plugin release, shown in the Plugin Manager list and details
      (max 15 characters).
  @{b}NOVADOCK_AUTHOR@{ub}
      Meaning: Author or publisher, shown by the Plugin Manager.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_DESCRIPTION@{ub}
      Meaning: One line describing what the tile does; the Plugin Manager
      wraps it over two lines of about 46 characters.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_LICENSE@{ub}
      Meaning: Licence name or identifier, shown after the version.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_WEBSITE@{ub}
      Meaning: Optional project page.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_FEATURES@{ub}
      Meaning: Comma-separated capabilities, from: meter (shows a value or
      bar), graph (history graph), click (reacts to clicks), popup (opens a
      popup or window), drop (accepts dropped Workbench icons), media
      (player transport controls), badge (a label on the tile's corner).
      Unknown words are displayed as written. Do not list settings or
      several tiles: the Plugin Manager reports those from NOVADOCK_OPTION
      and NOVADOCK_MULTIPLE.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_PROGRAM@{ub}
      Meaning: Installed executable file name, when it differs from the
      manifest's name.
  @{b}NOVADOCK_OPTION@{ub}
      Meaning: KEY
      : Label
      : TYPE
      : choices
      : default, up to twelve. TYPE is BOOL, CHOICE (comma-separated
      choices), UINT (min,max), TEXT, or FILE (text plus an ASL file
      chooser). The default must be valid for its type.

Lines that do not start with NOVADOCK_ are ignored (comments, DONOTWAIT and
other tool types). Unknown NOVADOCK_ keys are ignored so newer manifests
still work with this version. A text manifest beside the program takes
precedence over tool types in its icon.

@{b}What Preferences does with it@{ub}
-----------------------------

  * Drawers scanned: NovaDock:Plugins and, when Preferences runs from a
    development directory, PROGDIR:Plugins. A manifest whose program is
    missing is ignored.
  * A @{b}new@{ub} plugin is added to the working configuration @{b}disabled@{ub}, as
    PLUGIN=<Name>|Plugins/<file>|<ID>|<VIEW>|OFF|<MULTIPLE>, with its option
    schema. The General page title says how many were found. Nothing is
    written until the user adds the plugin and chooses Use or Save. A new
    plugin never starts by itself.
  * A plugin that is @{b}already configured@{ub} gets its option schema refreshed
    from the manifest, so an upgraded plugin can add, rename or drop
    settings. Values already stored by the user are kept.
  * IDs of bundled plugins (CLOCK, CPU, RAM, NET, ROADSHOW, MUSIC, EYES,
    DOPUS4, ARCHIVE, RUNNINGAPPS) cannot be claimed by a manifest.
  * A plugin is rejected, with the reason shown in the Organize title, if
    its manifest is invalid, needs a newer ABI, would exceed the 32
    third-party setting definitions, or the dock already has 24 items.

@{b}Reading the settings in the plugin@{ub}
----------------------------------

Use the option reader from the SDK. Defaults in code should match the
manifest's defaults:

    char city[32];
    nd_plugin_option_string(argv[1], "CITY", "Stockholm", city, sizeof(city));

Reread settings when an exchange returns ND_EVENT_SETTINGS. The SDK's
examples/hello.c and examples/Hello.ndplugin show the complete pattern.

OPTIONDEF= lines written by hand still work; a manifest simply generates
them.

@{b}Plugin Manager@{ub}
--------------

Preferences > Plugins lists every installed plugin with its version and dock
status. Selecting one shows its version and licence, author, capabilities,
dock status, program and description. Bundled plugins take this information
from NovaDock itself; third-party plugins from their manifest, so complete
NOVADOCK_VERSION, NOVADOCK_AUTHOR, NOVADOCK_DESCRIPTION and
NOVADOCK_FEATURES lines make a plugin look as finished as the bundled ones.

@{b}Import...@{ub} installs a package from its .ndplugin manifest and @{b}Remove...@{ub}
uninstalls a third-party plugin without tiles; see PLUGIN_SDK_QUICKSTART.md
for the package rules.

A plugin is active exactly when it has a tile in the dock. Right-click the
dock and choose Add to place a tile; use a tile's right-click menu for its
settings or to remove it. The Plugins page shows installed plugin
information and provides Import and Remove for third-party packages.
@endnode

@node PLUGIN_SETTINGS "Plugin settings"
@toc Main
@{b}Plugin settings@{ub}
===============

All user configuration is in the single novadock.cfg, not one file per
plugin. ENV:NovaDock/novadock.cfg is the session copy and
ENVARC:NovaDock/novadock.cfg is the permanent copy.

Open a plugin tile's settings from its right-click menu. Add plugins from
right-click -> Add on the dock. The Plugins page in Preferences lists
installed plugins and their details, with Import and Remove for third-party
packages. The dock can still be started independently from WBStartup.

Option metadata comes from built-in definitions or third-party OPTIONDEF
entries, not hidden external preference files. Supported editor fields:

  @{b}Field            Config type  UI@{ub}
  --------------------------------------------------------------------
  Toggle           BOOL         Yes/No cycle
  Choice           CHOICE       Cycle of listed choices
  Bounded integer  UINT         Validated text input
  Text             TEXT         Text input
  File             FILE         Editable path and a Select file button

Third-party plugins normally describe their settings themselves with a
manifest (NOVADOCK_OPTION lines, see @{"Self-describing plugins" link PLUGIN_MANIFEST}); Preferences
then writes the OPTIONDEF lines below automatically. Writing them by hand
still works:

    PLUGIN=Example|Plugins/Example|EXAMPLE|METER|ON
    OPTIONDEF=EXAMPLE|ENABLED|Enable widget|BOOL||YES
    OPTIONDEF=EXAMPLE|DISPLAY|Display mode|CHOICE|COMPACT,FULL|COMPACT
    OPTIONDEF=EXAMPLE|REFRESH|Refresh ticks|UINT|25,1000|100
    OPTION.EXAMPLE.ENABLED=YES
    OPTION.EXAMPLE.DISPLAY=FULL
    OPTION.EXAMPLE.REFRESH=100

A third-party executable should call nd_plugin_options_effective() and
provide a callback (void *ctx, const char *key, const char *value) returning
1 for accepted values and 0 for invalid values; see
include/nd_option_runtime.h and plugin_src/common/nd_option_runtime.c. The
helper reads only OPTION.EXAMPLE.* records and ignores unrelated fields.
ND_EVENT_SETTINGS is an optional additive event flag; existing ABI 1/2
message structures are unchanged. Plugins should reread settings when they
receive this event. Disabled plugins remain in the config on save.

The dock does not execute option strings as shell commands. Plugins must
validate external command mappings and file/device paths independently.
Third-party plugin-specific custom controls are not automatically available;
this editor supports the five schema types above. The manifest has no hook
to replace Edit settings with an external preferences executable.

The focused editor adapts to the Workbench screen height and scrolls when
its fields cannot fit. The manifest supports up to 12 option definitions.

@{b}Use@{ub} writes the session copy and @{b}Save@{ub} writes both session and permanent
copies, closes the editor, then asks the running dock and plugins to reload.
Cycle and string values are collected directly from the live GadTools
controls, so changes such as Clock Digital / Analog take effect without
restarting the dock. Invalid values leave the editor open with an error
title; write failures are reported separately.

The editor, save path and live reload have been compiled for 68k and
exercised on AmigaOS Workbench.

@{b}Save and recovery behavior@{ub}
--------------------------

NovaDock closes a complete .new file before replacing novadock.cfg, and
retains the preceding fully valid generation as novadock.cfg.bak. A
malformed current file is never copied over that backup. Effective loading
accepts only zero-error parses and tries ENV:, its .good and .bak
generations, ENVARC: and its generations, then packaged PROGDIR: settings
and their generations, and finally SYS:NovaDock/novadock.cfg.default. The
Diagnostics page identifies a selected backup as @{b}Recovered config@{ub}. Saving
the recovered working copy repairs the active file through the same
replacement path.

@{b}Bundled Eyes widget@{ub}
-------------------

The new EYES plugin uses the exact same built-in schema mechanism as Clock,
RAM, Network and MusicPlayer. Right-click the Eyes tile and choose @{b}Edit
settings...@{ub}. No extra configuration file or separate editor is installed.
See @{"Eyes plugin" link EYES_PLUGIN} for the full options.

Focused settings windows choose their visible row count from the Workbench
height. Network shows seven normal options; its Advanced button exposes
three sampling override controls. Taller screens fit these together without
a scrollbar; smaller screens keep scrolling so all options remain reachable.

For a complete SDK walkthrough, see @{"Creating your plugin settings window" link PLUGIN_SDK_SETTINGS}.
@endnode

@node RELEASING "Building and publishing releases"
@toc Main
@{b}Building and publishing releases@{ub}
================================

@{b}Sections@{ub}
  @{" Commands " link RELEASING 21}
  @{" Configure once " link RELEASING 55}
  @{" Finish and commit release inputs " link RELEASING 130}
  @{" Build, check and prepare " link RELEASING 151}
  @{" Review the manifest " link RELEASING 194}
  @{" Publish selected destinations " link RELEASING 216}
  @{" Combined release command " link RELEASING 254}
  @{" Upload source separately " link RELEASING 266}
  @{" Aminet and interrupted uploads " link RELEASING 296}
  @{" Validation and website updates " link RELEASING 325}

Run python3 scripts/release.py from the project root with Python 3.9 or
newer. tools/release.py is the same entry point and remains supported. Build
once, review the prepared manifest, then publish the same verified bytes to
each selected destination. No command deploys the website or creates hosted
repositories.

@{b}Commands@{ub}
--------

  @{b}plan@{ub}
      Purpose: Print ordered commands and target information
      Uploads: None
  @{b}build@{ub}
      Purpose: Run host tests, compile all native programs, package and
      validate
      Uploads: None
  @{b}check@{ub}
      Purpose: Verify current source, binaries, provenance, packages and
      checksums
      Uploads: None
  @{b}prepare@{ub}
      Purpose: Seal notes, destinations and exact assets in a manifest
      Uploads: None
  @{b}publish@{ub}
      Purpose: Preflight every selected destination, then publish
      Uploads: Explicit confirmation required
  @{b}release@{ub}
      Purpose: Prepare and publish, reusing an identical prepared manifest
      Uploads: Explicit confirmation required
  @{b}source@{ub}
      Purpose: Preflight or push a committed source branch
      Uploads: Separate source confirmation required

NovaDock has one available build target: Classic AmigaOS m68k, baseline
68000, AmigaOS 3.0 or later. Signing and notarization do not apply to this
target. Compilation and host checks are recorded separately from native
runtime checks. Complete docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md on your supported
configurations before publishing; a successful compiler invocation does not
certify runtime behaviour.

@{b}Configure once@{ub}
--------------

The default configuration is external to the project:
~/Development/tools/git-release/projects/novadock.json. This matches the
shared release-tool layout. Override it with NOVADOCK_RELEASE_CONFIG or
--config PATH. If the shared file is absent, an existing ignored
work/release-config.json is accepted for compatibility. Start from
examples/release-config.json and set the real private GitLab HTTPS and SSH
URLs in the external copy. Never commit local machine paths or credential
settings. The earlier nested URL format from
docs/release-targets.example.json remains accepted.

The public destination is https://github.com/retrocs-org/novadock. The tool
validates HTTPS and Git push URLs as the same repository, including nested
GitLab groups. Credential-bearing URLs, query strings, encoded paths,
traversal, custom ports and non-HTTPS API destinations are rejected. The
prepared manifest records the exact destinations; changing local
configuration after preparation blocks publication. Keep manifests and
receipts private if they identify an internal repository. They are not part
of the public source packages.

Allowed local build environment overrides are VBCC, VC, LHA, CC, PHP,
FLEXCAT, AUTHOR, AMINET_REPLACES and NOVADOCK_RELEASE_PYTHON. Use executable
paths for tool overrides. The selected Python interpreter is used for every
release step, including the shell build/staging scripts and host checks.
Without an override, the interpreter running release.py is used. Configure
vbcc/vasm/vlink with the AmigaOS m68k target and a compatible NDK. LHA must
be LHa for UNIX with archive creation support. PHP and a host C compiler are
needed for validation. The uploader defaults to support\@retrocs.org; set
uploader in the local JSON or pass --uploader to override it.

API credentials can come from environment variables or 1Password CLI,
matching the shared release configuration. The default provider reads
GITHUB_TOKEN or GITLAB_TOKEN. To use 1Password, configure a copied secret
reference or an exact vault/item/field lookup in the external file:

    "credentials": {
      "github": {
        "provider": "1password",
        "vault": "Your vault",
        "item": "github.com",
        "field": "your-org/novadock-rw"
      },
      "gitlab": {
        "provider": "1password",
        "reference": "op://vault/item/field"
      }
    }

Optional account selects a 1Password account. Use provider: "env" with
variable: "YOUR_TOKEN_VARIABLE" for a different environment variable. Values
in the JSON are references, never token contents. A configured provider is
used explicitly; an environment token does not silently override a 1Password
setting.

Secrets are read only for confirmed repository publication, held in memory
and redacted from release errors. They are not copied into environment
variables, notes, manifests or receipts. Build subprocesses receive no API
tokens or 1Password session/service-account credentials. Planning, checking,
preparation, source pushing and the build itself do not retrieve 1Password
secrets.

GitHub needs repository Contents write access; GitLab needs API access and a
role allowed to create releases and generic packages. SSH keys or Git
credential helpers provide separate Git push access. Configure/sign in to
the 1Password CLI before publication if using that provider. Do not put
literal tokens in command arguments, URLs, configuration, notes, source
files or shell history, and avoid shell tracing. Unknown or ambiguous secret
fields stop before uploads and their output is not displayed.

The repositories must already exist, and the internal GitLab project must
already be private with its generic package registry enabled. The tool does
not configure repository visibility, credentials, keys, DNS or hosting.

@{b}Finish and commit release inputs@{ub}
--------------------------------

Update VERSION and its CHANGELOG section, then refresh generated source
before committing:

    python3 tools/update_version.py
    python3 tools/update_site_chrome.py
    python3 tools/generate_catalog.py
    python3 tools/make_amigaguide.py
    python3 tools/generate_info_icons.py
    python3 tools/sync_github_releases.py --upcoming
    python3 tools/package_release.py

Review and commit the intended source. These generators are repeated during
build; if they change tracked inputs, finish the generated changes and
commit before building again. All selected source inputs must be tracked,
including website captions, documentation, manifests and SDK helpers.
Ignored or untracked build inputs cannot silently enter a publishable
release.

@{b}Build, check and prepare@{ub}
------------------------

    python3 scripts/release.py plan
    python3 scripts/release.py build
    python3 scripts/release.py check
    python3 scripts/release.py prepare

The default output directory is outputs/release. Use --output PATH on each
command to select another directory; it must be outside the source tree or
below ignored outputs/. build requires an empty directory and always
compiles fresh binaries. Preserve failed output as evidence and use a fresh
directory to retry. There is no unified skip-build option. The older
standalone packaging scripts remain available for local maintenance, but
their files are not automatically trusted as a publishable build.

For compatibility, prepare also runs build when its output has no build
record. For the explicit workflow above, it only seals already checked
artifacts. Use prepare --notes docs/RELEASE-NOTES.md to seal a reviewed
notes file instead of notes generated from CHANGELOG. This deliberately
changes the checksums and manifest; it is refused once the manifest has been
prepared.

Build records connect the exact source fingerprint and commit to all native
executables, compiler identity and artifact hashes. The fingerprint covers
the explicit source inventory, documentation, build tools, tests, plugin
metadata and website inputs. Generated downloads, compiled binaries, local
configuration and provenance records are excluded from their own input
fingerprint. The source ZIPs include build/provenance/amiga.json, identical
to the release's BUILD-INFO.json.

Changing any release input after building requires another build. Checks
reject stale fingerprints, changed binaries, stale source archive contents,
missing or unexpected assets, duplicate entries and symlinked files. Package
checks also compare the website's downloads and checksum list with the
release bytes. These checks establish matching provenance; they do not
promise bit-for-bit reproducibility across toolchains.

An extracted source tree may build and prepare local files with a null
commit. Publishing requires a clean, committed Git checkout at the source
root, with the same commit and tracked inputs as the build. Do not edit
build records to bypass this requirement.

@{b}Review the manifest@{ub}
-------------------

Review outputs/release/manifest.json, RELEASE-NOTES.md, BUILD-INFO.json and
the Aminet readme. release.json is a compatibility alias and must match
manifest.json. The manifest contains version/tag derived from VERSION, exact
source commit, source fingerprint, configured repository destinations, notes
and every asset hash. It is immutable: preparing again may confirm identical
data, but cannot replace it with different notes or destinations. Use a new
release directory for changed input.

The exact public asset inventory is:

  * NovaDock-VERSION.lha, its readme and LHA checksum file;
  * source ZIP and Plugin SDK ZIP;
  * PHP website ZIP and complete source/site ZIP;
  * RELEASE-NOTES.md, BUILD-INFO.json and SHA256SUMS.txt.

SHA256SUMS.txt covers every other public asset exactly once. Its own hash is
in the manifest. Local build-state, manifests, destination configuration and
upload receipts are not public assets.

@{b}Publish selected destinations@{ub}
-----------------------------

    python3 scripts/release.py publish --to gitlab,github --confirm-publish
    python3 scripts/release.py publish --to aminet --confirm-publish

Or select --to gitlab,github,aminet to preflight all three before any
upload. The default destinations are GitLab and GitHub. Destination names
must be distinct. Without --confirm-publish, publication is refused before
network access. The previous --execute flag remains an explicit confirmation
alias; --target github or --target gitlab remains available for older
operator commands.

Before any push or upload, the tool checks the manifest, current source and
binaries, clean commit, tokens, destination identity, existing assets,
release notes and remote tags. Annotated remote tags are peeled to their
source commit. A conflicting tag stops publication. It creates an annotated
local tag and pushes only that exact annotated tag object, without force or
automatic tag following, then verifies the remote commit. It does not push
branches or change default branches; the separate source command below is
used for branch publication.

GitHub uploads into a draft, verifies every returned SHA-256 digest and only
then publishes the release. Existing assets are reused only with matching
known hashes; unknown or conflicting digests, unexpected assets and changed
notes stop the run. Published releases missing assets are not silently
modified. Versions beginning with 0. are marked prerelease. No gh
installation is needed.

GitLab uses versioned generic-package paths. Each existing or uploaded file
is stream-downloaded and hashed before its link is advertised in a release.
Matching packages are reused; conflicting bytes, notes or links stop
publication. GitLab has no matching prerelease flag. No glab installation is
needed.

GitHub draft discovery checks the latest 100 releases before looking up the
tag; very old interrupted drafts may need manual inspection.

@{b}Combined release command@{ub}
------------------------

    python3 scripts/release.py release --to gitlab,github,aminet --confirm-publish

This prepares an unprepared build and then uses the same publisher. If no
build exists, preparation first performs the normal fresh build. An existing
prepared manifest is reused; the command cannot silently replace its notes,
tag annotation, assets or destinations. prepare --tag-message "NovaDock 0.1"
records a custom annotation in the manifest. Tags still derive from VERSION
so the website and asset URLs cannot drift from the application version.

@{b}Upload source separately@{ub}
------------------------

    python3 scripts/release.py source --to gitlab,github --dry-run
    python3 scripts/release.py source --to gitlab,github --confirm-push

This preflights every selected branch push before uploading to any
repository, pushes the reviewed commit ID without force or automatic tag
following, and verifies the resulting branch commit. It performs no
compilation, release-tag creation or binary upload. source_branch in the
external JSON selects the remote branch; an empty value uses the current
branch. The checkout must be clean with required source inputs tracked.
Tracked private configuration, build evidence or local output blocks it.

For a new extracted source tree, explicitly add --init. Initialization is
allowed only when all selected remotes have no refs and a Git
author/committer identity is configured. It stages only the selected public
source inventory, respects .gitignore and commits it. --commit-message
"Import NovaDock source" customizes that first commit. Existing repositories
are never reset or replaced. An unfinished repository with already staged
files must be reviewed before initialization can continue. If a selected
remote already has history, clone it and import changes there. source --init
--dry-run only checks and describes the import; it creates no metadata or
commit. Source publication still requires --confirm-push.

Generated website downloads are ignored by Git and rebuilt during packaging.
Private machine toolchain adapters belong in ignored work/ or an external
tools directory. Cross-builds always select the same public C sources as
native builds; private compatibility source overrides are not used.

@{b}Aminet and interrupted uploads@{ub}
------------------------------

Aminet submission uses anonymous FTP to main.aminet.net, directory /new,
with the uploader email as the contact. The adapter verifies the LHA CRC and
the prepared pair's hashes against the repository release. It sends
novadock.lha first and novadock.readme last. Submission success means
awaiting moderation, not publication on the public archive. Repository
publishing alone never submits to Aminet.

outputs/release/receipt.json records the manifest hash, destination
currently in progress and completed destinations. A different manifest
cannot reuse it. Rerun the same manifest for a failed repository
destination; verified matching assets are reused and successful releases are
preserved. No cross-service rollback or automatic network retry occurs.

Aminet also maintains aminet/upload-receipt.json, bound to that submission's
exact bytes. It records transfer progress before connecting and after each
file. A completed receipt prevents duplicate submission. An incomplete
receipt blocks automatic retry, including uncertain connection failures:
inspect the incoming server and receipt before deciding recovery. Do not
delete receipts to bypass the check. Re-preparing over an existing Aminet
upload receipt is refused.

The standalone tools/aminet_release.py retains its own offline prepare/check
and explicit upload commands and observes the same receipt rules. The
compatibility release.py aminet command checks locally; --execute routes
through the unified confirmed publisher.

@{b}Validation and website updates@{ub}
------------------------------

Ordinary tests use mocked APIs, fake FTP transfers and temporary Git
repositories; they never publish real tags or submit files. Run:

    python3 tests/test_release_tool.py
    python3 tests/test_release_provenance.py
    python3 tests/test_release_integration.py
    python3 tests/test_aminet_release.py

Exercise publication against dedicated test repositories before a production
release. Preserve build records and receipts as evidence of the release run.
After publishing, run python3 tools/sync_github_releases.py and redeploy the
PHP website to show actual release dates and assets. Publishing attaches the
website ZIP; it does not deploy it. Refreshing website inputs changes the
next build's fingerprint and cannot silently change an already prepared
release.

API references: GitHub releases
(https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/releases), GitLab generic packages
(https://docs.gitlab.com/user/packages/generic_packages/) and GitLab
releases (https://docs.gitlab.com/api/releases/). Aminet references: upload
instructions (https://wiki.aminet.net/Uploading_instructions) and readme
format (https://wiki.aminet.net/The_Readme_file).

Credential references: 1Password read command
(https://developer.1password.com/docs/cli/reference/commands/read/).
@endnode

@node RELEASE_CHECKLIST "Release checklist"
@toc Main
@{b}Release checklist@{ub}
=================

  * Set VERSION and its release date, update CHANGELOG, and regenerate
    version metadata.
  * Build all programs and run the host and native source checks.
  * Verify the LHA extracts correctly and matches the staged drawer.
  * Test a fresh install, updating existing settings and uninstalling with
    settings retained.
  * Open Preferences, Help, Diagnostics and each bundled app on supported
    screen sizes.
  * Check the low-resource preset, configuration recovery and Save/Use
    behavior.
  * Test Clipboard search, empty results, pinning and copying; test Apps
    title selection.
  * Check drawers, icon loading, keyboard navigation and auto-hide.
  * Verify optional hardware and network dependencies on the target
    configuration.
  * Review release notes, uploader details, repository targets and download
    checksums.
  * Publish from a clean committed checkout using the release tool.

Host tests and compilation do not replace interactive checks on the
supported AmigaOS configurations. Record test results privately and retain
any open issues.
@endnode

@node NETWORK_MIGRATION "Upgrading Network settings"
@toc Main
@{b}Upgrading Network settings@{ub}
==========================

Roadshow connection controls are provided by the @{"Network plugin" link NETWORK_PLUGIN}. The
standalone Roadshow plugin and executable alias are no longer included.
Existing Roadshow tiles migrate to Network when settings are loaded. Legacy
option values remain available as a fallback; updates keep user settings.

The update installer removes the retired program, icon and manifest. For a
manual update, quit NovaDock first, remove those old Roadshow files from the
Plugins drawer, install the new binaries and restart the dock.
@endnode
